sec.p:
The order parameters are the diquark condensates $\Phi _{\mathrm {L,R}}$ defined by
(2.1) \begin{equation} (\Phi_{\rm L})_a^A \sim \epsilon_{abc} \epsilon_{ABC} \langle (q_{\rm L})_b^B C (q_{\rm L})_c^C \rangle, \label{eq2.1} \end{equation} 

(2.2) \begin{equation} (\Phi_{\rm R})_a^i \sim \epsilon_{abc} \epsilon_{ABC} \langle (q_{\rm R})_b^B C (q_{\rm R})_c^C \rangle,\label{eq2.2} \end{equation} 

where $q_{\mathrm {L,R}}$ stand for the left- (right)-handed quarks with the indices $a,b,c = 1,2,3 = r,g,b$ being those of colors while $A,B,C=1,2,3 = u,d,s$ are those of flavors, and $C$ is the charge conjugation operator. The diquark Cooper pairs are induced by an attractive interaction via one-gluon exchange in the s-wave color antisymmetric channel. The flavor indices must also be antisymmetric because of Pauli’s principle. The most generic form of the GL free energy for uniform matter is
(2.3) \begin{align} \Omega_0 &=a_{0{\mathrm{L}}}{\rm Tr}[\Phi_{\rm L}^{\dagger} \Phi_{\rm L}] + a_{0{\rm R}}{\rm Tr}[\Phi_{\rm R}^{\dagger} \Phi_{\rm R}] \nonumber\\ &\quad + b_{1{\rm L}}\left({\rm Tr}[\Phi_{\rm L}^{\dagger} \Phi_{\rm L}]\right)^2 + b_{1{\rm R}}\left({\rm Tr}[\Phi_{\rm R}^{\dagger} \Phi_{\rm R}]\right)^2 + b_{2{\rm L}}{\rm Tr}[(\Phi_{\rm L}^{\dagger} \Phi_{\rm L})^2]\nonumber\\ &\quad + b_{2{\rm R}}{\rm Tr}[(\Phi_{\rm R}^{\dagger} \Phi_{\rm R})^2]+ b_3{\rm Tr}[\Phi_{\rm L}^{\dagger} \Phi_{\rm L} \Phi_{\rm R}^{\dagger} \Phi_{\rm R}] + b_4{\rm Tr}[\Phi_{\rm L}^{\dagger} \Phi_{\rm L}]{\rm Tr}[ \Phi_{\rm R}^{\dagger} \Phi_{\rm R}].\label{eq2.3} \end{align} 

The color symmetry $SU(3)_{\mathrm { C}}$, the flavor symmetry $SU(3)_{\mathrm {L}} \times SU(3)_{\mathrm { R}}$, the $U(1)_{\mathrm {B}}$ symmetry, and the $U(1)_{\mathrm {A}}$ symmetry act on $\Phi _{\mathrm {L}}$ and $\Phi _{\mathrm {R}}$ as
(2.4) \begin{align} \Phi_{\rm L} &\to e^{i\theta_{\rm B}+i\theta_{\rm A}} U_{\rm C} \Phi_{\rm L} U_{\rm L}, \\ \Phi_{\rm R} &\to e^{i\theta_{\rm B}-i\theta_{\rm A}} U_{\rm C} \Phi_{\rm R} U_{\rm R},\label{eq2.4} \end{align} 

with $e^{i\theta _{\mathrm {B}}} \in U(1)_{\mathrm { B}}$, $e^{i\theta _{\mathrm {A}}} \in U(1)_{\mathrm { A}}$, $U_{\mathrm {C}} \in SU(3)_{\mathrm { C}}$, and $U_{\mathrm {L,R}} \in SU(3)_{\mathrm { L,R}}$. Hereafter, we omit the last two terms since they can be neglected in the high baryon density region1 . Although the interactions induced by the perturbative gluon exchanges do not distinguish the positive diquark condensates from the negative one, the non-perturbative instanton effects do. It was found that the state with positive parity is favored compared to the one with negative parity as a ground state [12,280]. We then choose the positive-parity state in what follows,
(2.5) \begin{equation}\label{eq2.5} \Phi_{\mathrm{L}}=-\Phi_{\rm R} \equiv \Phi. \end{equation}

For later convenience, let us explicitly show the structure of indices of the $3 \times 3$ matrix field $(\Phi)_a{}^A$
(2.6) \begin{equation}\label{eq2.6} \Phi = \left(\begin{matrix} \Phi_{gb}{}^{ds} & \Phi_{gb}{}^{su} & \Phi_{gb}{}^{ud} \\ \Phi_{br}{}^{ds} & \Phi_{br}{}^{su} & \Phi_{br}{}^{ud} \\ \Phi_{rg}{}^{ds} & \Phi_{rg}{}^{su} & \Phi_{rg}{}^{ud} \end{matrix}\right), \end{equation} 

with the color indices $a,b,c=1,2,3=gb, br,rg$ and the flavor indices $A,B,C=ds, su,ud$. Let us next give a GL free energy for the inhomogeneous condensate $\Phi $, which includes gradient energies and gluon fields to $\Omega _0$ given in Eq. (2.3),
(2.7) \begin{align} \Omega &= {\mathrm{Tr}}\left[ \frac{1}{4 \lambda_3} F_{ij}^2 + \frac{\varepsilon_3}{2} F_{0i}^2 + K_3\mathcal{D}_i \Phi^{\dagger} \mathcal{D}_i \Phi \right] + V, \nonumber\\ V&=\alpha {\rm Tr}\left(\Phi^{\dagger} \Phi \right) + \beta_1 \left[{\rm Tr}(\Phi^{\dagger}\Phi)\right]^2 +\beta_2 {\rm Tr} \left[(\Phi^{\dagger}\Phi)^2\right] + \frac{3\alpha^2}{4(\beta_1+3\beta_2)},\label{eq2.7} \end{align} 

where $\lambda _{3}$ is a magnetic permeability, and $\varepsilon _{3}$ is a dielectric constant for gluons, $i,j=1,2,3$ are indices for space coordinates, and the covariant derivative and the field strength of gluons are defined by
(2.8) \begin{equation} \mathcal{D}_{\mu} \Phi = \partial_{\mu} \Phi - i g_{\mathrm{s}} A^a_{\mu} T^a \Phi,\label{eq2.8} \end{equation} 

(2.9) \begin{equation} F_{\mu \nu} = \partial_{\mu}A_{\nu}-\partial_{\nu}A_{\mu}-ig_{\rm s}[A_{\mu},A_{\nu}]. \label{eq2.9} \end{equation} 

Here, $\mu ,\nu $ are indices for spacetime coordinates and $g_{\mathrm {s}}$ stands for the $SU(3)_{\mathrm {C}}$ coupling constant. The coupling constants $\alpha , \beta _{1,2}, K_{0,3}$ are obtained from the weak-coupling calculations, which are valid at a sufficiently high density as [181,143]
(2.10) \begin{equation} \alpha = 4 N(\mu) \log \frac{T}{T_{\mathrm{c}}}, \label{eq2.10} \end{equation} 

(2.11) \begin{equation} \beta_1 = \beta_2 = \frac{7\zeta(3)}{8(\pi T_{\rm c})^2}\, N(\mu)\equiv \beta, \label{eq2.11}\end{equation} 

(2.12) \begin{equation} K_3 = \frac{7\zeta(3)}{12(\pi T_{\rm c})^2}N(\mu), \label{eq2.12} \end{equation} 

(2.13) \begin{equation} \lambda_0 = \epsilon_0 = 1,\quad \lambda_3 = \epsilon_3 = 1, \label{eq2.13} \end{equation} 

where $\mu $ stands for the quark chemical potential. We have introduced the density of state $N(\mu)$ at the Fermi surface
(2.14) \begin{equation}\label{eq2.14} N(\mu) = \frac{\mu^2}{2\pi^2}. \end{equation}

The Lagrangian (2.7) has the same symmetry as QCD except for the chiral symmetry, which is spontaneously broken to the diagonal one, reflecting the fact that the positive parity state is favored in the ground state as Eq. (2.5). When only $\Phi _{\mathrm {L}}$ ($\Phi _{\mathrm {R}}$) condenses, the color $SU(3)_{\rm C}$ and the flavor $SU(3)_{\mathrm {L}}$ ($SU(3)_{\mathrm {R}}$) are spontaneously broken down to their diagonal subgroup $SU(3)_{\rm C+L}$ ($SU(3)_{\mathrm {C+R}}$). The ground state can be found by minimizing the potential energy in Eq. (2.7),
(2.15) \begin{equation}\label{eq2.15} \Phi = {\rm diag}(\Delta_{\rm CFL}, \Delta_{\rm CFL}, \Delta_{\rm CFL}),\quad \Delta_{\rm CFL} \equiv \sqrt{-\frac{\alpha}{8\beta}}, \end{equation} 

up to a color and flavor rotation. This is the so-called color–flavor-locking (CFL) phase of the QCD. The chiral symmetry is spontaneously broken as $SU(3)_{\mathrm {L}} \times SU(3)_{\mathrm { R}} \to SU(3)_{{\mathrm { L}}+{\mathrm { R}}}$ as explained above. Since the condensate matrix $\Phi $ is proportional to the identity matrix, the diagonal symmetry of the color $SU(3)_{\mathrm {C}}$ and the flavor $SU(3)_{{\mathrm {L}}+{\mathrm { R}}}$ remains as the genuine symmetry in the CFL phase.

Let us see the detailed structure of the spontaneous symmetry breaking in CFL. Firstly, recall the actions of the symmetries on the order parameter $\Phi $,
(2.16) \begin{equation}\label{eq2.16} \Phi \rightarrow e^{i\theta_{\mathrm{B}}} U_{\rm C} \Phi U_{\rm F}, \end{equation} 

with $e^{i\theta _{\mathrm { B}}} \in U(1)_{\mathrm { B}}$, $U_{\mathrm {C}} \in SU(3)_{\mathrm { C}}$, and $U_{\mathrm {F}} \in SU(3)_{{\mathrm { L}}+{\mathrm { R}}}$. There is a redundancy in the action of the symmetries, and the actual symmetry group is given by
(2.17) \begin{equation}\label{eq2.17} G=\frac{SU(3)_{\rm C} \times SU(3)_{\rm F} \times U(1)_{\rm B}} {(\mathbb{Z}_3)_{{\rm C}+{\rm B}} \times (\mathbb{Z}_3)_{{\rm F}+{\rm B}}}, \end{equation} 

where the discrete groups are defined by
(2.18) \begin{equation} (\mathbb{Z}_3)_{{\rm C}+{\rm B}} :\left(\omega^k\textbf{1}_3,\textbf{1}_3,\omega^{-k}\right)\in SU(3)_{\rm C} \times SU(3)_{\rm F} \times U(1)_{\rm B}, \label{eq2.18} \end{equation} 

(2.19) \begin{equation} (\mathbb{Z}_3)_{{\rm F}+{\rm B}} :\left(\textbf{1}_3,\omega^k\textbf{1}_3,\omega^{-k}\right)\in SU(3)_{\rm C} \times SU(3)_{\rm F} \times U(1)_{\rm B}, \label{eq2.19} \end{equation} 

with $k=0,1,2$ and $\omega $ being
(2.20) \begin{equation}\label{eq2.20} \omega \equiv e^{2\pi i/3}. \end{equation} 

Note that the discrete groups can be rearranged as
(2.21) \begin{equation}\label{eq2.21} (\mathbb{Z}_3)_{{\mathrm{C}}+{\rm B}} \times (\mathbb{Z}_3)_{{\rm F}+{\rm B}} \simeq (\mathbb{Z}_3)_{{\rm C}+{\rm F}} \times (\mathbb{Z}_3)_{{\rm C}-{\rm F}+{\rm B}} \end{equation} 

with
(2.22) \begin{equation} (\mathbb{Z}_3)_{{\rm C}+{\rm F}} : \left(\omega^k\textbf{1}_3,\omega^{-k}\textbf{1}_3,1\right)\in SU(3)_{\rm C} \times SU(3)_{\rm F} \times U(1)_{\rm B}, \label{eq2.22} \end{equation} 

(2.23) \begin{equation} (\mathbb{Z}_3)_{{\rm C}-{\rm F}+{\rm B}} : \left({\omega^k\bf 1}_3,\omega^{k}\textbf{1}_3,\omega^{-2k}\right)\in SU(3)_{\rm C} \times SU(3)_{\rm F} \times U(1)_{\rm B}. \label{eq2.23} \end{equation} 

In the ground state, the full symmetry group $G$ is spontaneously broken down to
(2.24) \begin{equation}\label{eq2.24} H = \frac{SU(3)_{{\mathrm{C}}+{\rm F}}\times (\mathbb{Z}_3)_{{\rm C}-{\rm F}+{\rm B}}}{(\mathbb{Z}_3)_{{\rm C}+{\rm B}} \times (\mathbb{Z}_3)_{{\rm F}+{\rm B}}} \simeq \frac{SU(3)_{{\rm C}+{\rm F}}}{(\mathbb{Z}_3)_{{\rm C}+{\rm F}}}. \end{equation} 

Thus, we find the order parameter space of the ground state as
(2.25) \begin{equation}\label{eq2.25} \frac{G}{H} \simeq \frac{SU(3)_{{\rm C}-{\rm F}}\times U(1)_{\rm B}}{(\mathbb{Z}_3)_{{\rm C}-{\rm F}+{\rm B}}} \simeq U(3)_{{\rm C}-{\rm F}+{\rm B}}. \end{equation} 

This $U(3)$ manifold is parametrized by 9 would-be NG modes, among which 8 are eaten by the gluons via the Higgs mechanism and only one massless scalar field (referred to as the $H$ boson) associated with the $U(1)_{\mathrm {B}}$ symmetry breaking remains in the physical spectrum2 .

Including the broken $U(1)_{\mathrm {A}}$ symmetry and the chiral symmetry, the full order parameter space (OPS) in the CFL phase is given by
(2.26) \begin{equation}\label{eq2.26} \mathcal{M}_{\rm CFL} = U(3)_{{\rm C}-{\rm F}+{\rm B}} \times U(3)_{{\rm L}-{\rm R}+{\rm A}}. \end{equation} 

The GL Lagrangian (2.7) describes only the first part, while the second part describing the NG modes associated with the broken $U(1)_{\mathrm {A}}$ and the chiral symmetry is discussed in Sect. 2.2.2.

The spectrum of the GL theory (2.7) can be found by expanding the order parameter $\Phi $ around the ground state given in Eq. (2.15) as
(2.27) \begin{equation}\label{eq2.27} \Phi(x) = \Delta_{\mathrm{CFL}}\textbf{1}_3 + \frac{\phi_1(x) + i \varphi(x)}{\sqrt{2}}\textbf{1}_3 + \frac{\phi_8^a(x) + i \zeta^a(x)}{\sqrt{2}}T^a. \end{equation} 

The fields proportional to $\textbf {1}_3$ and $T^a$ belong to the singlet ($\textbf {1}$) and adjoint ($\textbf {8}$) representations of the CFL symmetry given in Eq. (2.24). The small fluctuations $\zeta ^a(x)$ are absorbed by the gluons and $\varphi (x)$ remains the massless $U(1)_{\mathrm {B}}$ NG mode (phonon). The masses of the gluons, $\phi _1$, and $\phi _8^a$ are given respectively as follows:
(2.28) \begin{equation}\label{eq2.28} m_{\mathrm{g}}^2 = 2\lambda_3 g_{\rm s}^2 \Delta_{\rm CFL}^2 K_3, \quad m_{1}^2 = - \frac{2\alpha}{K_3}, \quad m_{8}^2 = \frac{4\beta \Delta_{\rm CFL}^2}{K_3}. \end{equation} 

From this, together with Eq. (2.13), we find the following relation
(2.29) \begin{equation}\label{eq2.29} m_{\rm g} \sim \sqrt{\lambda_3} g_{\rm s} \mu, \quad m_1 = 2m_8 \sim 2\Delta_{\rm CFL}. \end{equation} 

Note that $g_{\mathrm { s}}\mu \gg \Delta _{\mathrm { CFL}}$ at the high density limit, so that we have
(2.30) \begin{equation}\label{eq2.30} \kappa_{1,8} \equiv \frac{m_{1,8}}{m_{\mathrm{g}}} \ll 1. \end{equation} 

This implies that the CFL phase is in the type I superconductor [144].3

So far, we have considered the CFL phase at the asymptotically high density where all the quark masses $m_{\mathrm {u,d,s}}$ are negligible compared to the baryon chemical potential $\mu $. In this subsection, let us consider the effect of the finite non-zero strange quark mass while the masses of the u and d quarks are kept at zero,
(2.31) \begin{equation}\label{eq2.31} 0\simeq m_{\mathrm{u,d}} < m_{\rm s} \ll \mu. \end{equation} 

The effects of non-zero quark masses become important at smaller baryon chemical potentials. It was found [179] that the non-zero quark mass together with the $\beta $-equilibrium and the electric charge neutrality changes the CFL phase to the modified CFL (mCFL) phase where the color–flavor-locking symmetry is further broken as
(2.32) \begin{equation}\label{eq2.32} SU(3)_{{\mathrm{C}}+{\rm L}+{\rm R}} \to U(1)^2. \end{equation} 

The important difference between the CFL and mCFL phases is that the quark chemical potentials $\mu _{\mathrm { u}},\mu _{\mathrm { d}}, \mu _{\rm s}$ take different values. Hence, there appear difference between the Fermi momenta and the gaps of the diquark condensation take different values as [179]
(2.33) \begin{equation}\label{eq2.33} \Delta_{\mathrm{ud}} > \Delta_{\rm ds} >\Delta_{\rm us}. \end{equation} 

This is responsible for the symmetry breaking in Eq. (2.32). The correction to quadratic order to the GL potential in Eq. (2.7) was obtained as [178,179]
(2.34) \begin{equation} \delta V = \frac{2}{3}\varepsilon\ {\rm Tr}\,\left[\Phi^{\dagger}\Phi\right] + \varepsilon\ {\rm Tr}\,\left[\Phi^{\dagger} X_3\Phi\right],\label{eq2.34} \end{equation} 

(2.35) \begin{equation} \varepsilon = N(\mu)\frac{m_{\rm s}^2}{\mu^2} \log\frac{\mu}{T_{\rm c}},\label{eq2.35} \end{equation} 

(2.36) \begin{equation} X_3 = {\rm diag}\left(0,\frac{1}{2},-\frac{1}{2}\right).\label{eq2.36} \end{equation}

The first term in Eq. (2.34) can be absorbed into the definition of $\alpha $ as
(2.37) \begin{equation}\label{eq2.37} \alpha' \equiv \alpha + \frac{2}{3}\varepsilon. \end{equation} 

If we ignore the second term in Eq. (2.34), all the results in the previous sections are still valid under the understanding of the replacement $\alpha $ with $\alpha '$ in all equations. For instance, $\Delta _{\mathrm {CFL}}$ is replaced with
(2.38) \begin{equation}\label{eq2.38} \Delta_\varepsilon \equiv \sqrt{- \frac{\alpha'}{8\beta}}. \end{equation} 

Therefore, an essential difference from the massless case is given rise to by the second term in Eq. (2.34). Since the term is sufficiently small if $m_{\mathrm {s}} \ll \mu $, we will treat it as a perturbation in Sect. 5.2.

Let us next include the electromagnetic interaction that is realized as a $U(1)$ action from the right-hand side on $\Phi $ as
(2.39) \begin{equation}\label{eq2.39} \Phi \to \Phi e^{i e T^{\mathrm{EM}}},\quad T^{\rm EM} = \sqrt{\frac{2}{3}}\,T^8 = {\rm diag}\left(-\frac{2}{3},\frac{1}{3},\frac{1}{3}\right). \end{equation} 

The covariant derivative of $\Phi $ is changed from Eq. (2.8) to
(2.40) \begin{equation}\label{eq2.40} \mathcal{D}_{\mu} \Phi = \partial_{\mu} \Phi - i g_{\mathrm{s}} A^a_{\mu} T^a \Phi - i e A^{\rm EM}_{\mu} \Phi T^{\rm EM}, \end{equation} 

and the field strength is
(2.41) \begin{equation}\label{eq2.41} F^{\rm EM}_{\mu\nu} = \partial_{\mu} A_{\nu}^{\rm EM} - \partial_{\nu} A_{\mu}^{\rm EM}. \end{equation} 

The GL thermodynamic potential is modified as
(2.42) \begin{align} \Omega &={\rm Tr}\left[\frac{1}{4 \lambda_3} F_{ij}^2 + \frac{\varepsilon_3}{2} F_{0i}^2 + K_3\mathcal{D}_i \Phi^{\dagger} \mathcal{D}_i \Phi \right] \\ &\quad + \frac{1}{4 \lambda_0} (F^{\rm EM}_{ij}){}^2 + \frac{\varepsilon_0}{2} (F^{\rm EM}_{0i}){}^2+ V,\label{eq2.42} \end{align} 

where $V$ is the same as the one in Eq. (2.7) and $\lambda _{0}$ and $\varepsilon _0$ are the magnetic permeability and the dielectric constant for the electromagnetic field.

There is mixing between $A_i^{\mathrm {EM}}$ and $A_i^8$ in the CFL phase, since the electric charge is proportional to $T_8$ as shown in Eq. (2.38) [12]. Let us first rescale the gauge fields by
(2.43) \begin{equation}\label{eq2.43} \mathcal{A}_i^a = \frac{1}{\sqrt{\lambda_3}} A_i^a,\quad \mathcal{A}_i^{\mathrm{EM}} = \frac{1}{\sqrt{\lambda_0}} A_i^{\rm EM}. \end{equation} 

This redefinition changes the kinetic terms of the electromagnetic fields and the gluons into the canonical forms as $- \frac {1}{4}{\rm Tr}[(\mathcal {F}_{ij})^2] - \frac {1}{4} (\mathcal {F}_{ij}^{\rm EM})^2$. Then, the covariant derivatives are rewritten as
(2.44) \begin{equation}\label{eq2.44} \mathcal{D}_i \Phi = \partial_i \Phi - i \sqrt{\lambda_3} g_{\mathrm{s}} \mathcal{A}_i^a T^a \Phi - i \sqrt{\lambda_0} e \Phi \mathcal{A}_i^{\rm EM}T^{\rm EM}. \end{equation}

When the order parameter $\Phi $ is diagonal (in particular in the ground state), the covariant derivative acting on the $\Phi $ can be rewritten as
(2.45) \begin{equation} \mathcal{D}_i \Phi = \partial_i \Phi - i \sqrt{\lambda_3} g_{\mathrm{s}} \mathcal{A}^{\tilde a}_i T^{\tilde a} \Phi - i \sqrt{\lambda_0} g_{\rm M} {\mathcal{A}}_i^{\rm M} T^{\rm EM} \Phi, \label{eq2.45}\end{equation} 

(2.46) \begin{equation} g_{\rm M} \equiv \sqrt{e^2 + \frac{3}{2}\frac{\lambda_3}{\lambda_0}g_{\rm s}^2}, \label{eq2.46} \end{equation} 

(2.47) \begin{equation} T^{\rm M} \equiv T^{\rm EM} \label{eq2.47} \end{equation} 

with $\tilde {a} = 1,2,\ldots ,7$, in terms of rotated gauge fields defined by
(2.48) \begin{equation} {\mathcal{A}}_i^{\mathrm{M}} \equiv \cos \chi {\mathcal{A}}_i^{\rm EM} + \sin \chi {\mathcal{A}}_i^8, \label{eq2.48}\end{equation} 

(2.49) \begin{equation} {\mathcal{A}}_i^{0} \equiv - \sin \chi {\mathcal{A}}_i^{\rm EM} + \cos \chi {\mathcal{A}}_i^8, \label{eq2.49} \end{equation} 

with the mixing angle defined by
(2.50) \begin{equation}\label{eq2.50} \tan \chi = \frac{\sqrt{\frac{3}{2}\frac{\lambda_3}{\lambda_0}} g_{\rm s}}{e}. \end{equation} 

We have rewritten $T^{\mathrm { EM}}$ by $T^{\mathrm {M}}$ in order to remember that it acts on $\Phi $ from its left while $T^{\mathrm {EM}}$ acts on its right. One can see from Eq. (2.44) that ${\mathcal {A}}_i^{\mathrm {M}}$ couples to $\Phi $ and so is massive in the ground state [see Eq. (2.51), below], while ${\mathcal {A}}_i^{0}$ decouples from $\Phi $ and so is massless. However, one should note that this rotation is valid only when $\Phi $ is diagonal, and it is meaningless for general $\Phi $.

The ground state of the theory is unchanged with respect to the case without the electromagnetic interaction:
(2.51) \begin{equation}\label{eq2.51} \langle \Phi_{a}^{\ A}\rangle = \Delta_{\mathrm{CFL}} \delta_{a}^{\ A}. \end{equation} 

Because of the nontrivial mixing, the masses of gluons are modified in the CFL phase as
(2.52) \begin{equation}\label{eq2.52} m^{2}_{{\rm g},\tilde a}=2 \lambda_3 g_{\rm s}^{2}\Delta_{\rm CFL}^{2}K_{3},\quad m^{2}_{{\rm g},{\rm M}}=\frac{4}{3}\lambda_0 g_{\rm M}^{2}\Delta_{\rm CFL}^{2}K_{3}, \end{equation} 

where $\tilde {a}$ runs from 1 to 7 and $m_{{\mathrm {g}},{\mathrm { M}}}$ is the mass of the gluon corresponding to the generator $T^{8}$.

One of the important effects of introducing electromagnetic interactions is the explicit breaking of the $SU(3)_{\mathrm {F}}$ flavor symmetry, even if we still consider all quarks to be massless. Introducing electromagnetic interactions is equivalent to gauging the $U(1)$ subgroup generated by $T^{\mathrm {EM}}$ inside the $SU(3)_{\mathrm {F}}$ flavor symmetry. Therefore, the flavor $SU(3)$ is explicitly broken as
(2.53) \begin{equation}\label{eq2.53} SU(3)_{\mathrm{F}} \xrightarrow{T^{\small{\rm EM}}} SU(2)_{\rm F}\times U(1)_{\small{\rm EM}}, \end{equation} 

where $SU(2)_{\mathrm { F}}$ is a subgroup of $SU(3)_{\mathrm {F}}$ commuting with $T_{\mathrm {EM}}$. The full set of symmetries of the CFL phase of QCD with electromagnetic interactions is thus given by the following (apart from the chiral symmetry):
(2.54) \begin{equation}\label{eq2.54} G=U(1)_{\rm B}\times U(1)_{{\small{\rm EM}}}\times SU(3)_{\rm C}\times SU(2)_{\rm F}. \end{equation} 

Apart from the unbroken gauge $U(1)$ symmetry, the CFL ground state Eq. (2.50) has the following diagonal color–flavor symmetry:
(2.55) \begin{equation}\label{eq2.55} H_{\small{\rm EM}} = SU(2)_{{\mathrm{C}}+{\rm F}}. \end{equation} 

This reduced symmetry is crucial to understanding the property of non-Abelian vortices with electromagnetic coupling.

In this section, let us discuss the time-dependent Ginzburg–Landau Lagrangian (TDGL) for the CFL phase [4]. The time-independent part of the TDGL is the same as Eq. (2.7). Then, the TDGL is given by
(2.56) \begin{align} \mathcal{L} &={\rm Tr}\left[-\frac{\varepsilon_3}{2} F_{0i}F^{0i} - \frac{1}{4 \lambda_3} F_{ij}F^{ij} \right] - \frac{\varepsilon_0}{2} (F^{\rm EM})_{0i}(F^{{\rm EM}})^{0i} - \frac{1}{4 \lambda_0} (F^{\rm EM})_{ij}(F^{{\rm EM}})^{ij} \nonumber\\ &\quad + {\rm Tr}\left[2 i \gamma K_0 (\mathcal{D}_0 \Phi^{\dagger} \Phi - \Phi^{\dagger} \mathcal{D}_0 \Phi) + K_0 \mathcal{D}_0\Phi^{\dagger} \mathcal{D}^0\Phi + K_3\mathcal{D}_i \Phi^{\dagger} \mathcal{D}^i\Phi \right] - V,\label{eq2.56} \end{align} 

with
(2.57) \begin{equation}\label{eq2.57} K_0 = 3K_3. \end{equation} 

$K_0$ has not been calculated in the literature, but can be derived following the same procedure as in Refs. [2,287].

At zero temperature, there is no dissipation. However, the dissipation is present at finite temperature, which cannot be incorporated in the Lagrangian. Instead, the Euler–Lagrange equation can be modified as
(2.58) \begin{equation}\label{eq2.58} K_{\mathrm{D}} \frac{\partial \Phi}{\partial t} = \frac{\delta \mathcal{L}}{\delta \Phi^*} \end{equation} 

with a real GL parameter $K_{\mathrm { D}} = K_{\mathrm { D}}(T,\mu)$, which has not been calculated from microscopic theory. Formally, this term can be obtained by replacing $2 i K_0 \gamma \partial /\partial t$ by $(2 i K_0 \gamma - K_{\mathrm {D}}) \partial /\partial t$ in the Euler–Lagrange equation of the Lagrangian in Eq. (2.55).

Let us construct the $U(1)_{\mathrm {B}}$ Noether current:
(2.59) \begin{align} j_0^{\rm B} &= \gamma K_0 {\rm Tr} (\Phi^{\dagger} \Phi) + 2 i K_0 {\rm Tr} \left[ \mathcal{D}_0 \Phi^{\dagger} \Phi - \Phi^{\dagger} \mathcal{D}_0 \Phi\right], \nonumber\\ j^{\rm B}_i &= 2 i K_3 {\rm Tr} (\mathcal{D}_i\Phi^{\dagger} \Phi - \Phi^{\dagger} \mathcal{D}_i \Phi). \label{eq2.59} \end{align} 

The Noether current satisfies the continuity equation:
(2.60) \begin{equation}\label{eq2.60} \partial_0 j^{\rm B}_0 - \partial_i j^{\rm B}_i = 0. \end{equation} 

The Noether charge defined by
(2.61) \begin{equation}\label{eq2.61} Q^{\rm B} = \int d^3 x j^{\rm B}_0 \end{equation} 

is a conserved quantity.

When we ignore the second derivative with respect to the time coordinate, the charge density is reduced as
(2.62) \begin{equation}\label{eq2.62} j_0^{\rm B} \sim \gamma K_0\,{\rm Tr}\,\Phi^{\dagger} \Phi \equiv \rho, \end{equation} 

where $\rho $ is called the superfluid density. Then, the superfluid velocity is defined by dividing the spatial components of the Noether current by the superfluid density:
(2.63) \begin{equation}\label{eq2.63} J^{\mathrm{B}}_i = \frac{2 i K_3}{\gamma K_0} \frac{{\rm Tr} (\mathcal{D}_i\Phi^{\dagger} \Phi- \Phi^{\dagger} \mathcal{D}_i \Phi)}{{\rm Tr} (\Phi^{\dagger} \Phi)}. \end{equation} 

The vorticity is defined by the rotation of the superfluid velocity
(2.64) \begin{equation}\label{eq2.64} \omega_i = \frac{1}{2} \epsilon_{ijk} \partial_j J^{\rm B}_k. \end{equation}

The conjugate momentum of $\Phi $ is
(2.65) \begin{equation}\label{eq2.65} \Pi_\Phi = K_0 \left(\mathcal{D}_0\Phi^{\dagger} - 2 i \gamma \Phi^{\dagger} \right), \end{equation} 

and the Hamiltonian is given by
(2.66) \begin{align} \mathcal{H} &={\mathrm{Tr}}\left[\frac{\varepsilon_3}{2} (F_{0i})^2 + \frac{1}{4 \lambda_3} (F_{ij})^2 + K_0 \left| \mathcal{D}_0\Phi\right|^2 + K_3\left|\mathcal{D}_i\Phi\right|^2 \right]\nonumber\\ &\quad + \frac{\varepsilon_0}{2} (F^{\rm EM}_{0i})^2 + \frac{1}{4 \lambda_0} (F_{ij}^{\rm EM})^2 +V. \label{eq2.66} \end{align}

Let us first take the phase of the diquark condensate $qq$ as the Nambu–Goldstone field. An effective action takes the form
(2.67) \begin{equation}\label{eq2.67} \mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{B}} = 12 f_{\rm B}^2 \left[\left(\partial_0\varphi_{\rm B} + 2\mu\right)^2 - v_{\rm B}^2 (\partial_i\varphi_{\rm B})^2 \right], \end{equation} 

where the coefficients were calculated [333,334] as
(2.68) \begin{equation}\label{eq2.68} f_{\rm B}^2 = \frac{3\mu^2}{8\pi^2},\quad v_{\rm B}^2 = \frac{1}{3}, \end{equation} 

and $\mu $ is the quark chemical potential. Note that the time derivative is not $\partial _0$ but $\partial _0 + 2\mu $. This is needed for the effective theory to have the same symmetry as QCD. This can be understood as follows. The Lagrangian of QCD in the medium is given by
(2.69) \begin{equation}\label{eq2.69} \mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{QCD}} = \bar{q} (i \gamma_{\nu}\partial^\nu) q + \mu q^{\dagger} q = \bar{q} (i\gamma^\nu D_{\nu}) q, \end{equation} 

where we introduced a covariant derivative $D_{\nu } = \partial _{\nu } + i B_{\nu }$ with a fictitious (spurion) gauge field $B_{\mu }$, which corresponds to the baryon current [128]. $B_{\mu }$ will be set to $B_{\mu } = (\mu ,0,0,0)$ in the end. The QCD Lagrangian is invariant under the $U(1)_{\mathrm {B}}$ rotation
(2.70) \begin{equation}\label{eq2.70} q \to e^{i\theta_{\rm B}} q,\quad \varphi_{\rm B} \to \varphi_{\rm B} + 2 \theta_{\rm B},\quad B_{\nu} \to B_{\nu} - \partial_{\nu} \theta_{\rm B}. \end{equation} 

We now require the low energy effective field to have this symmetry; one then reaches the Lagrangian (2.66).

There is another derivation of the effective theory in gauge invariant fashion [331] by taking the phase of the gauge invariant operator $qqqqqq$ as the $U(1)_{\mathrm {B}}$ Nambu–Goldstone field. In Ref. [331], it was found that the effective field theory takes the form
(2.71) \begin{equation}\label{eq2.71} \mathcal{L}_{\rm B}' = \frac{N_{\rm C}N_{\rm F}}{12\pi^2} \left[\left(\partial_0\varphi_{\rm B} - 6 \mu\right)^2 - (\partial_i\varphi_{\rm B})^2\right]^2. \end{equation} 

Expanding this Lagrangian and taking terms to the quadratic order in the derivative with $N_{\mathrm {C}} = N_{\mathrm { F}} = 3$, one reproduces the same Lagrangian as Eq. (2.66).

In this subsection, we study the chiral Lagrangian describing the Nambu–Goldstone modes for chiral symmetry breaking, i.e., the CFL mesons and the $\eta '$ meson. So far, we have not considered the spontaneous symmetry breaking of the axial $U(1)_{\mathrm {A}}$ symmetry, since it is explicitly broken by quantum effects (the chiral anomaly). Instantons induce vertices that break the $U(1)_{\mathrm {A}}$ symmetry, since instantons flip the chirality of fermions. The instanton effects becomes arbitrarily small at the high density limit, so that the chiral anomaly is suppressed. Therefore, at high density, the spontaneous broken $U(1)_{\mathrm {A}}$ symmetry introduces a light meson, namely the $\eta '$ meson. Accordingly, there appears an order parameter corresponding to this. Including the axial $U(1)_{\mathrm {A}}$ symmetry, the flavor symmetry $SU(3)_{\mathrm {L}} \times SU(3)_{\mathrm { R}} \times U(1)_{\mathrm { A}}$ of QCD acts on the gauge invariant
(2.72) \begin{equation}\label{eq2.72} \Sigma \equiv \Phi_{\rm L}^{\dagger} \Phi_{\rm R}, \end{equation} 

as
(2.73) \begin{equation}\label{eq2.73} \Sigma \to g_{\rm L}^{\dagger} \Sigma g_{\rm R} e^{2 i \theta_{\rm A}},\quad (g_{\rm L}, g_{\rm R}, e^{2 i \theta_{\rm A}}) \in SU(3)_{\rm L} \times SU(3)_{\rm R} \times U(1)_{\rm A}. \end{equation} 

While discrete groups are not well discussed in the literature, here we discuss them in more detail. There is a redundancy of discrete groups in this action, and the actual flavor symmetry is
(2.74) \begin{equation}\label{eq2.74} G_{\rm F} =\frac{SU(3)_{\rm L} \times SU(3)_{\rm R} \times U(1)_{\rm A}} {({\mathbb{Z}}_3)_{{\rm L}+{\rm A}} \times ({\mathbb{Z}}_3)_{{\rm R}+{\rm A}}} \simeq \frac{SU(3)_{\rm L} \times SU(3)_{\rm R} \times U(1)_{\rm A}} {({\mathbb{Z}}_3)_{{\rm L}+{\rm R}}\times ({\mathbb{Z}}_3)_{{\rm L}-{\rm R}+{\rm A}}}, \end{equation} 

where the redundant discrete groups are
(2.75) \begin{equation} (\mathbb{Z}_3)_{{\rm L}+{\rm A}}\ :\ \left(\omega^k\textbf{1}_3,\textbf{1}_3,\omega^{-k}\right) \in SU(3)_{\rm L} \times SU(3)_{\rm R} \times U(1)_{\rm A},\label{eq2.75} \end{equation} 

(2.76) \begin{equation} (\mathbb{Z}_3)_{{\rm R}+{\rm A}}\ :\ \left(\textbf{1}_3,\omega^{k}\textbf{1}_3,\omega^{-k}\right) \in SU(3)_{\rm L} \times SU(3)_{\rm R} \times U(1)_{\rm A},\label{eq2.76}\end{equation} 

(2.77) \begin{equation} (\mathbb{Z}_3)_{{\rm L}+{\rm R}}\ :\ \left(\omega^k\textbf{1}_3,\omega^{-k}\textbf{1}_3,1\right) \in SU(3)_{\rm L} \times SU(3)_{\rm R} \times U(1)_{\rm A},\label{eq2.77} \end{equation} 

(2.78) \begin{equation} (\mathbb{Z}_3)_{{\rm L}-{\rm R}+{\rm A}}\ :\ \left({\omega^k\bf 1}_3,\omega^{k}\textbf{1}_3,\omega^{-2k}\right) \in SU(3)_{\rm L} \times SU(3)_{\rm R} \times U(1)_{\rm A},\label{eq2.78} \end{equation} 

with $\omega $ in Eq. (2.20). Here the suffix ${\mathrm {A}}$ always implies $U(1)_{\mathrm {A}}$. With the same group structures as Eqs. (2.24) and (2.25), the unbroken symmetry in the ground state $\Sigma \sim \textbf {1}_3$ is
(2.79) \begin{equation}\label{eq2.79} H_{\mathrm{F}}= \frac{SU(3)_{{\rm L}+{\rm R}} \times ({\mathbb{Z}}_3)_{{\rm L}-{\rm R}+{\rm A}}} {({\mathbb{Z}}_3)_{{\rm L}+{\rm A}} \times ({\mathbb{Z}}_3)_{{\rm R}+{\rm A}}} \simeq \frac{SU(3)_{{\rm L}+{\rm R}}}{({\mathbb{Z}}_3)_{{\rm L}+{\rm R}}} \end{equation} 

and the order parameter space is found to be
(2.80) \begin{equation}\label{eq2.80} \frac{G_{\rm F}}{H_{\rm F}}\simeq \frac{SU(3)_{\rm L-R} \times U(1)_{\rm A} }{({\mathbb{Z}}_3)_{{\rm L}-{\rm R}+{\rm A}}}\simeq U(3)_{{\rm L}-{\rm R}+{\rm A}}. \end{equation}

The low-energy effective field theory for the $U(3)_{{\mathrm { L}}-{\rm R}+{\mathrm { A}}}$ NG modes, the chiral Lagrangian at high baryon density and at zero temperature, was first obtained in Ref. [71]. Let us first restrict the generic field $\Sigma $ in Eq. (2.71) by the constraints4
(2.81) \begin{equation}\label{eq2.81} \Sigma = \Phi_{\rm L}^{\dagger} \Phi_{\rm R},\quad \Phi_{\rm L}\Phi_{\rm L}^{\dagger} = \Phi_{\rm R} \Phi_{\rm R}^{\dagger} =\mathbf{1}_3. \end{equation} 

Furthermore, we decompose $\Sigma $ into $SU(3)$ and $U(1)$ parts as
(2.82) \begin{equation}\label{eq2.82} \Sigma = \tilde{\Sigma} e^{i\varphi_{\mathrm{A}}},\quad \tilde{\Sigma} = \Phi_{\rm L}^{\dagger} \Phi_{\rm R}. \end{equation} 

Then, the chiral Lagrangian is given by [71]
(2.83) \begin{align} \mathcal{L} &= \frac{f_{\pi}^2}{4}{\rm Tr} \left[\nabla_0\tilde{\Sigma}\nabla_0\tilde{\Sigma}^{\dagger} - v_{\pi}^2 \partial_i\tilde{\Sigma}\partial_i\tilde{\Sigma}^{\dagger}\right] + \frac{3f^2}{4}\left[\partial_0V\partial_0V^* - v_{\eta'}^2 \partial_iV \partial_iV^*\right] \\ &\quad + \left(A {\rm Tr}\left[M\tilde{\Sigma}^{\dagger}\right] V^* + {\rm h.c.}\right) + \left(B_1 {\rm Tr}[ M\tilde{\Sigma}^{\dagger}]{\rm Tr}[M\tilde{\Sigma}^{\dagger}]V \right.\nonumber\\ &\quad +\left. B_2 {\rm Tr} [ M\tilde{\Sigma}^{\dagger} M\tilde{\Sigma}^{\dagger}] V + B_3 {\rm Tr} [ M\tilde{\Sigma}^{\dagger}]{\rm Tr}[M^{\dagger}\tilde{\Sigma}] + {\rm h.c.}\right) + \cdots, \label{eq2.83} \end{align} 

where we have omitted the electromagnetic interaction. Here $M={\mathrm { diag}}(m_{\mathrm { u}},m_{\mathrm { d}},m_{\mathrm { s}})$ is a mass matrix, $\tilde {\Sigma }$ stands for the octet chiral field, and $V$ is the axial $U(1)_{\mathrm {A}}$ field
(2.84) \begin{equation}\label{eq2.84} \tilde{\Sigma} = \exp\left(i\frac{\pi^a T^a}{f_{\pi}}\right),\quad V = e^{i\varphi_{\rm A}} = \exp\left(i\frac{2\eta'}{\sqrt{6}f}\right), \end{equation} 

with $f_{\pi }$ and $f$ being the octet and singlet decay constants. The covariant derivative of the time derivative for $\tilde {\Sigma }$ is defined by [47]
(2.85) \begin{equation}\label{eq2.85} \nabla_0 \tilde{\Sigma} = \partial_0 \tilde{\Sigma} + i \left(\frac{MM^{\dagger}}{2\mu}\right) \tilde{\Sigma} - i \tilde{\Sigma} \left(\frac{M^{\dagger} M}{2\mu}\right). \end{equation} 

The decay constants and speed of mesons are obtained as [333,334]
(2.86) \begin{equation}\label{eq2.86} f_{\pi}^2 = \frac{21-8\log2}{18}\,\frac{\mu^2}{2\pi^2}, \quad f^2 = \frac{3}{4}\, \frac{\mu^2}{2\pi^2},\quad v_{\pi}^2 = v_{\eta'}^2 = \frac{1}{3}. \end{equation} 

The mechanism of chiral symmetry breaking in the high baryon density is very different from that at low densities. Indeed, the dominant contribution is not the usual $\langle \bar \psi \psi \rangle $ but a four fermion operator $\langle (\bar \psi \psi)^2\rangle \sim \langle \psi \psi \rangle ^2$. Thus, dominant contributions from the quark mass terms appear as quadratic terms of $M$ in the chiral Lagrangian, namely the terms proportional to $B_i$. These coefficients are obtained as [333,334,303]
(2.87) \begin{equation}\label{eq2.87} B_1 = - B_2 = \frac{3\Delta_{\mathrm{CFL}}^2}{4\pi^2},\quad B_3 = 0, \end{equation} 

with $\Delta _{\mathrm { CFL}}$ being the gap in the quasiparticle spectrum. The terms linear in $M$ appear when $\langle \bar \psi \psi \rangle $ is not zero. Although they vanish at the asymptotically high density limit, they appear through non-perturbative effects, instantons [301,233,335,336,302]. The coefficient $A$ is calculated by Ref. [302] to the one-instanton level as
(2.88) \begin{equation}\label{eq2.88} A = C_N \frac{8\pi^4}{3}\frac{\Gamma(6)}{3^6}\left[\frac{3\sqrt2\pi}{g_{\mathrm{s}}}\Delta_{\rm CFL} \left(\frac{\mu^2}{2\pi^2}\right)\right]^2 \left(\frac{8\pi^2}{g_{\rm s}^2}\right)^6\left(\frac{\Lambda}{\mu}\right)^{12}\Lambda^{-3}, \end{equation} 

with $C_N = 0.466 \exp (-1.679 N_{\rm C})1.34^{N_{\rm F}}/(N_{\rm C}-1)!(N_{\rm C}-2)!$. Note that $A$ is related to the chiral condensate by $A= - \langle \bar {\psi }\psi \rangle /2$ [302].

From the chiral Lagrangian (2.83), one can read the masses of meson spectra. For instance, when all the quark masses are equal, $M = m \textbf {1}_3$, setting $\Sigma =\textbf {1}_3$, we have
(2.89) \begin{equation} \mathcal{L}_{\eta'}= \frac{1}{2}(\partial_0\eta')^2 - \frac{v_{\eta'}^2}{2}(\partial_i \eta')^2 - V_{1-{\mathrm{inst}}}(\eta'), \label{eq2.89} \end{equation} 

(2.90) \begin{equation} V_{1-{\rm inst}}(\eta') = \left(\frac{2Am}{f^2} + \frac{4m^2B}{f^2}\right) (\eta')^2 + \cdots.\label{eq2.90} \end{equation} 

Thus the mass of $\eta '$ is
(2.91) \begin{equation}\label{eq2.91} m_{\eta'}^2\big|_{1-{\mathrm{inst}}} = \frac{4A}{f^2}m + \frac{8B}{f^2}m^2. \end{equation} 

Therefore, in the chiral limit, $\eta '$ is massless at the one-instanton level [302]. The $\eta '$-mass in the chiral limit is generated by the two-instanton contribution. Although it is hard to evaluate it, the potential for $\eta '$ can be easily read as [302]
(2.92) \begin{equation}\label{eq2.92} V_{\mathrm{sym.}}(\varphi_{\rm A}) = -6mA \cos \varphi_{\rm A} - 12 m^2 B \cos \varphi_{\rm A} - 2C \cos 3\varphi_{\rm A}, \end{equation} 

where we have set the $\theta $ angle of QCD to zero and $B_1=-B_2=B$. The first term is the one-instanton contribution and the last term is the two-instanton contribution. We leave $C$ as a free parameter in this work. “sym.” in the subscript indicates the flavor symmetric limit $m_{\mathrm {u}} = m_{\mathrm { d}} = m_{\mathrm { s}} = m$.

Before closing this section, let us show the original derivation of the above chiral Lagrangian by Ref. [71]. Let $\Phi _{\mathrm {L}}$ and $\Phi _{\mathrm {R}}$ be coset fields for $SU(3)_{\mathrm {C}} \times SU(3)_{\mathrm { L}}$ and $SU(3)_{\mathrm {C}} \times SU(3)_{\mathrm { R}}$, respectively:
(2.93) \begin{equation}\label{eq2.93} \Phi_{\rm L}\Phi_{\rm L}^{\dagger} = \Phi_{\rm R} \Phi_{\rm R}^{\dagger} =\mathbf{1}_3, \quad \det \Phi_{\rm L} = \det \Phi_{\rm R} = 1. \end{equation} 

Since an effective theory of QCD should have the same symmetry as QCD, the effective Lagrangian derived in Ref. [71] is of the form
(2.94) \begin{align} \mathcal{L} &= - \frac{f_{\pi}^2}{4}{\rm Tr}\left[\left(\Phi_{\rm R}\partial_0 \Phi_{\rm R}^{\dagger} - \Phi_{\rm L}\partial_0 \Phi_{\rm L}^{\dagger}\right)^2 - v_{\pi}^2\left(\Phi_{\rm R}\partial_i \Phi_{\rm R}^{\dagger} - \Phi_{\rm L}\partial_i \Phi_{\rm L}^{\dagger}\right)^2\right] \nonumber\\ &\quad -C_{\alpha} \frac{f_{\pi}^2}{4} {\rm Tr}\left[\left(\Phi_{\rm R}\partial_i \Phi_{\rm R}^{\dagger} + \Phi_{\rm L}\partial_i \Phi_{\rm L}^{\dagger} + 2 i g_{\rm s}A_0\right)^2 - v_{\pi}^2 \left(\Phi_{\rm R}\partial_i \Phi_{\rm R}^{\dagger} + \Phi_{\rm L}\partial_i \Phi_{\rm L}^{\dagger} + 2 i g_{\rm s}A_i\right)^2\right]\nonumber\\ &\quad -\frac{3f^2}{4} \left(|\partial_0V|^2 - v_{\eta'}^2 |\partial_iV|^2\right) - \frac{1}{4}{\rm Tr}\left[F_{\mu\nu}F^{\mu\nu}\right].\label{eq2.94} \end{align} 

Since we are interested in the low-energy dynamics, we integrate out the gluons $A_{\mu }$, which are heavy. This can be done by neglecting the kinetic term of the gluons. Then, in this Lagrangian, the gluon is just an auxiliary field and can be eliminated by its equation of motion. After eliminating the gluon, we are left with the chiral Lagrangian
(2.95) \begin{align} \mathcal{L} &= - \frac{f_{\pi}^2}{4}{\mathrm{Tr}}\left[\left(\Phi_{\rm R}\partial_0 \Phi_{\rm R}^{\dagger} - \Phi_{\rm L}\partial_0 \Phi_{\rm L}^{\dagger}\right)^2 - v_{\pi}^2\left(\Phi_{\rm R}\partial_i \Phi_{\rm R}^{\dagger} - \Phi_{\rm L}\partial_i \Phi_{\rm L}^{\dagger}\right)^2\right] \nonumber\\ &\quad -\frac{3f^2}{4} \left(|\partial_0V|^2 - v_{\eta'}^2 |\partial_iV|^2\right)\nonumber\\ &=\frac{f_{\pi}^2}{4}{\rm Tr}\left[\partial_0\Sigma\partial_0\Sigma^{\dagger} - v_{\pi}^2 \partial_i\Sigma\partial_i\Sigma^\dagger\right] - \frac{3f^2}{4} \left(|\partial_0V|^2 - v_{\eta'}^2 |\partial_iV|^2\right). \label{eq2.95} \end{align}

Let us discuss $U(1)_{\mathrm {B}}$ superfluid vortices. The phases of diquark condensates can be parametrized as
(3.1) \begin{equation}\label{eq3.1} \Phi_{\rm L} = e^{i\theta_{\rm A} +i\theta_{\rm B}}\Delta_{\rm CFL} \textbf{1}_3,\quad \Phi_{\rm R} = e^{-i\theta_{\rm A} +i\theta_{\rm B}}\Delta_{\rm CFL} \textbf{1}_3. \end{equation} 

The phases $\varphi _{\mathrm {A}}$ and $\varphi _{\mathrm {B}}$ are the phases of the $U(1)_{\mathrm {A}}$ and $U(1)_{\mathrm {B}}$ symmetries, respectively. Both phase symmetries $U(1)_{\mathrm {B}}$ and $U(1)_{\mathrm {A}}$ are spontaneously broken when the diquarks are condensed, while the $U(1)_{\mathrm {A}}$ is also broken explicitly by the chiral anomaly, namely the instanton effects. The spontaneous breaking of $U(1)_{\mathrm {B}}$ and $U(1)_{\mathrm {A}}$ gives rise to $U(1)_{\mathrm {B}}$ and $U(1)_{\mathrm {A}}$ global vortices, respectively.

The $U(1)_{\mathrm {B}}$ global vortices can be easily found in Eq. (2.7) by requiring the condensate matrix to be proportional to the unit matrix $\Phi (x) = \phi (x) \textbf {1}_3/\sqrt 3$. Then, the Lagrangian is reduced to the following linear sigma model for the complex scalar field $\phi (x)$:
(3.2) \begin{align} \mathcal{L}_{U(1)_{\mathrm{B}}} &= K_0 \left(|\partial_0\phi|^2 - v_{\rm B}^2 |\partial_i\phi|^2\right) - \left[ \alpha |\phi|^2 + \frac{4\beta}{3} |\phi|^4 \right] + \frac{3\alpha^2}{16\beta}, \\ v_{\rm B} &\equiv \sqrt{\frac{K_3}{K_0}} = \frac{1}{\sqrt{3}}.\label{eq3.2} \end{align} 

The effective field $\phi $ develops the VEV $\langle \phi \rangle = \sqrt {3} \Delta _{\mathrm {CFL}}$ in the ground state, so that the $U(1)_{\mathrm {B}}$ is spontaneously broken. Since the first homotopy group is nontrivial,
(3.3) \begin{equation}\label{eq3.3} \pi_1[U(1)_{\rm B}] \simeq {\mathbb{Z}}, \end{equation} 

there exist topologically stable $k$ vortices in general with $k \in {\mathbb {Z}}$.

We make a standard ansatz for the axially symmetric $k$ vortex strings, which are infinitely long straight lines, say, along the $x_3$ axis: (3.4) \begin{equation}\label{eq3.4} \phi (r,\theta)= \sqrt{3}\Delta_{\mathrm{CFL}} f_{\rm B}(r) e^{ik\theta}, \end{equation}
with the cylindrical coordinates $(r,\theta ,z)$, i.e., $x^1 + i x^2 \equiv r e^{i\theta }$. A numerically obtained profile function $f_{\mathrm {B}}$ is shown in Fig. 2. Fig. 2.
A profile function $f_{\mathrm {B}}(r)$ of a minimally winding ($k=1$) $U(1)_{\mathrm {B}}$ vortex. The parameters are $\alpha = -1$ and $\beta = 2$ for simplicity.

The circulation of the superfluid velocity in Eq. (2.62) obeys the famous Onsager–Feynman quantization
(3.5) \begin{equation}\label{eq3.5} c_B = \oint d x^i J^{\mathrm{B}}_i = 2 \pi \frac{4K_3}{\gamma K_0} k \end{equation} 

with the vortex number $k \in \pi _1[U(1)_{\mathrm { B}}]$.

The Hamiltonian density with respect to the amplitude function $f_{\mathrm {B}}(r)$ is given by
(3.6) \begin{equation}\label{eq3.6} {\mathcal{H}}_{U(1)_{\rm B}} = 3\Delta_{\rm CFL}^3\left(K_3 f_{\rm B}'{}^2 + \left(\frac{K_3k^2}{r^2} + \alpha \right) f_{\rm B}^2 + 4\beta\Delta_{\rm CFL}^2f_{\rm B}^4\right). \end{equation} 

The $k$ static global string solution is obtained by solving the following equation of motion:
(3.7) \begin{equation}\label{eq3.7} K_3 \left(f_{\mathrm{B}}'' + \frac{f_{\rm B}'}{r}\right) - \frac{k^2K_3}{r^2} f_{\rm B} - \left(\alpha + 8\beta \Delta_{\rm CFL}^2 f_{\rm B}^2\right)f_{\rm B} = 0, \end{equation} 

with the boundary conditions $f_{\mathrm { B}}(0) = 0$ and $f_{\rm B}(\infty) \to 1$. The tension of the string logarithmically diverges as
(3.8) \begin{equation}\label{eq3.8} T_{U(1)_{\mathrm{B}}} = 6\pi \Delta_{\rm CFL}^2 K_3 k^2 \log \frac{L}{\xi} + \cdots, \end{equation} 

where $\cdots $ stands for a finite contribution to the tension, $L$ is an IR cutoff scale representing the system size and $\xi \sim m_1^{-1}$ is a UV cutoff representing the size of the vortex core.

Asymptotic solutions of a minimally winding vortex are given by
(3.9) \begin{equation} f_{\mathrm{B}} = c_1 (r/\sqrt{K_3}) + \cdots,\qquad\qquad r \to 0, \label{eq3.9} \end{equation} 

(3.10) \begin{equation} f_{\rm B} = 1 - \frac{2}{-\alpha (r/\sqrt{K_3})^2} + \cdots,\qquad r \to \infty.\label{eq3.10} \end{equation} 

Here the constant $c_1$ is known to be $c_1 = 0.58\cdots $ [355].

Here, we discuss color-magnetic flux tubes [180] that are non-topological because of a trivial homotopy group5
(3.12) \begin{equation}\label{eq3.12} \pi_1[SU(3)_{\mathrm{C}}] = 0 \end{equation} 

and consequently are unstable. A color magnetic flux is generated by one generator of the $SU(3)_{\mathrm { C}}$ color group. Among all possible generators, $T_8$ gives the lightest fluxes, because it can be mixed with the electromagnetic $U(1)_{\mathrm {EM}}$ acting on $\Phi $ from its right. The corresponding massive gauge field is given in Eq. (2.47).

To construct the vortex, we make the ansatz that all the off-diagonal elements of $\Phi $ are zero and denote
(3.13) \begin{equation}\label{eq3.13} \Phi(x) = {\mathrm{diag}} \left(\phi_1(x),\frac{\phi_2(x)}{\sqrt2},\frac{\phi_2(x)}{\sqrt 2}\right). \end{equation} 

Then, the Hamiltonian density is reduced as
(3.14) \begin{equation} \tilde{\mathcal{H}} = \frac{1}{4 }(\mathcal{F}^{\rm M}_{ij})^2 + |\mathcal{D}_i \phi_1|^2 + |\mathcal{D}_i \phi_2|^2 + \tilde{V},\label{eq3.14}\end{equation} 

(3.15) \begin{equation}\tilde{V} = \alpha \left(|\phi_1|^2 + |\phi_2|^2\right) + \beta \left(|\phi_1|^2 + |\phi_2|^2\right)^2 + \beta \left(|\phi_1|^4 + \frac{|\phi_2|^4}{2}\right),\label{eq3.15} \end{equation} 

with
(3.16) \begin{equation} \mathcal{D}_i \phi_1 = \partial_i \phi_1+ i \frac{2g_{\rm M}}{3} \mathcal{A}_i^{\rm M} \phi_1,\label{eq3.16}\end{equation} 

(3.17) \begin{equation} \mathcal{D}_i \phi_2 = \partial_i \phi_2 - i \frac{g_{\rm M}}{3} \mathcal{A}_i^{\rm M} \phi_2,\label{eq3.17}\end{equation} 

(3.18) \begin{equation}\mathcal{F}^{\rm M}_{ij} = \partial_i \mathcal{A}_j^{\rm M} - \partial_j \mathcal{A}_i^{\rm M},\label{eq3.18} \end{equation} 

and $g_{\rm M} = \sqrt {e^2 + 3\lambda _3g_{\rm s}^2/2\lambda _0}$ as defined in Eq. (2.45). In the ground state, the fields develop the VEV as $\langle \phi _1\rangle = \Delta _{\mathrm {CFL}}$ and $\langle \phi _2\rangle = \sqrt {2} \Delta _{\mathrm {CFL}}$, so that the $A^{\mathrm {M}}_{\mu }$ in Eq. (2.47) is massive and $A^0_{\mu }$ in Eq. (2.48) remains massless, as discussed before.

Now, we are ready to construct the magnetic flux tube by making the axially symmetric ansatz by
(3.19) \begin{align} \phi_1(r,\theta) &= f_1(r)e^{-2i\theta},\quad \phi_2(r,\theta) = f_2(r) e^{i\theta}, \\ A_i^{\mathrm{M}} (r,\theta) &= -\frac{3 \epsilon_{ij}}{\tilde g} \frac{x^j}{r^2}\chi(r),\label{eq3.19} \end{align} 

with the boundary condition
(3.20) \begin{equation}\label{eq3.20} f_1(0) = f_2(0) = \chi(0) = 0,\quad f_1(\infty) = f_2(\infty) = \chi(\infty) = 1. \end{equation} 

The magnetic flux that this vortex carries can be calculated as
(3.21) \begin{equation}\label{eq3.21} \int d^2x\ \mathcal{F}^{\rm M}_{12} = \frac{3}{g_{\rm M}} \int d^2x\ \frac{\chi'}{r} = \frac{6\pi}{g_{\rm M}}. \end{equation} 

This solution was studied in Ref. [180]. The flux in Eq. (3.21) is quantized but the solution is unstable and decays by turning on the other components of gauge fields.

For later convenience, let us set $\lambda _0 = \lambda _3 = 1$ and decompose the flux in Eq. (3.21) into gluon $SU(3)_{\rm C}$ and electromagnetic $U(1)_{\mathrm {EM}}$ parts as
(3.22) \begin{equation}\label{eq3.22} F_{12}^8 = F^{\rm M}_{12} \sin \zeta,\quad F^{\rm EM}_{12} = F^{\rm M}_{12} \cos \zeta, \end{equation} 

and take the limit $\zeta = \pi /2$, namely the limit where the electromagnetic interaction is ignored. Then we find that this flux tube has the pure color-magnetic flux
(3.23) \begin{equation}\label{eq3.23} \Phi^8_{\mathrm{A}} = \int d^2x\ F_{12}^8 = \frac{4\sqrt{3}\pi}{g_{\rm s}}. \end{equation} 

Note that $F_{12}^8$ is not a gauge invariant quantity but ${\rm Tr}\,[(F_{12})^2] = (F_{12}^8)^2$ is gauge invariant.

In this subsection, we explain the so-called non-Abelian vortices, which are the most stable vortices in the CFL phase [35]. For simplicity, here we omit the mixing of the color and electric charges by simply setting $e=0$, which we take into account in Sect. 5.3. We consider only static configurations. Therefore, we study solutions to the Euler–Lagrange equations associated with the Lagrangian
(3.24) \begin{align} \mathcal{L} &= {\mathrm{Tr}}\left[- K_3\mathcal{D}_i \Phi^{\dagger} \mathcal{D}_i \Phi - \frac{F_{ij}^2}{4 \lambda_3} \right] - \alpha\,{\rm Tr}\left(\Phi^{\dagger} \Phi \right) \\ &\quad - \beta \left(\left[{\rm Tr}(\Phi^{\dagger}\Phi)\right]^2 - {\rm Tr}\left[(\Phi^{\dagger}\Phi)^2\right]\right) + \frac{3\alpha^2}{16\beta},\label{eq3.24} \end{align} 

where we discarded the terms irrelevant for the discussions below. In the rest of this section, we will set $\lambda _3 = 1$. In order to get a static straight vortex string along the $x_3$-axis, we make the following ansatz:
(3.25) \begin{equation} \Phi(r,\theta) = \Delta_{\mathrm{CFL}} \left(\begin{matrix} e^{i\theta} f(r) & 0 & 0 \\ 0 & g(r) & 0 \\ 0 & 0 & g(r)\end{matrix}\right),\label{eq3.25} \end{equation} 

(3.26) \begin{equation} A_i(r,\theta) = \frac{\epsilon_{ij}x^j}{g_{\rm s}r^2} \left(1-h(r)\right) \left(\begin{matrix} -\frac{2}{3} & 0 & 0\\ 0 & \frac{1}{3} & 0\\ 0 & 0 & \frac{1}{3}\end{matrix}\right).\label{eq3.26} \end{equation} 

Similarly, we can take
(3.27) \begin{equation} \Phi(r,\theta) = \Delta_{\rm CFL} \left(\begin{matrix} g(r) & 0 & 0 \\ 0 & e^{i\theta} f(r) & 0 \\ 0 & 0 & g(r) \end{matrix}\right),\label{eq3.27} \end{equation} 

(3.28) \begin{equation} A_i(r,\theta) = \frac{\epsilon_{ij}x^j}{g_{\rm s}r^2} \left(1-h(r)\right) \left(\begin{matrix} \frac{1}{3} & 0 & 0\\ 0 & -\frac{2}{3} & 0\\ 0 & 0 & \frac{1}{3} \end{matrix}\right), \label{eq3.28} \end{equation} 

or
(3.29) \begin{equation} \Phi(r,\theta) = \Delta_{\rm CFL} \left(\begin{matrix} g(r) & 0 & 0 \\ 0 & g(r) & 0 \\ 0 & 0 & e^{i\theta} f(r)\end{matrix}\right),\label{eq3.29}\end{equation} 

(3.30) \begin{equation} A_i(r,\theta) = \frac{\epsilon_{ij}x^j}{g_{\rm s}r^2} \left(1-h(r)\right) \left(\begin{matrix} \frac{1}{3} & 0 & 0\\ 0 & \frac{1}{3} & 0\\ 0 & 0 & -\frac{2}{3} \end{matrix}\right).\label{eq3.30} \end{equation} 

These vortices were first found by Balachandran, Digal, and Matsuuta in Ref. [35]. They call them semi-superfluid non-Abelian vortices. For either case, the boundary conditions for the profile functions $f,g,h$ at spatial infinity should be determined in such a way that the configurations reach the ground state. Therefore, we impose the boundary condition
(3.31) \begin{equation}\label{eq3.31} f(\infty) = 1,\quad g(\infty) = 1,\quad h(\infty) = 0, \end{equation} 

where the last condition implies that we are in the pure gauge at infinity. We also need to fix the values of the fields at the origin, which ensures that the solutions are regular there:
(3.32) \begin{equation}\label{eq3.32} f(0) = 0,\quad g'(0) = 0,\quad h(0) = 1. \end{equation}

The decomposition of the $U(3)$ action in Eq. (3.25) to the $U(1)_{\mathrm {B}}$ and $SU(3)_{\mathrm {C}}$ actions can be found as
(3.33) \begin{equation}\label{eq3.33} \Phi = \Delta_{\rm CFL} \left(\begin{matrix} e^{i\theta} f(r) & & \\ & g(r) & \\ & & g(r)\end{matrix}\right) = \Delta_{\rm CFL} e^{\frac{i\theta}{3}} \left(\begin{matrix} e^{\frac{2i\theta}{3}} f(r)& &\\ & e^{-\frac{i\theta}{3}} g(r) &\\ & & e^{-\frac{i\theta}{3}} g(r) \end{matrix}\right). \end{equation} 

At the end $\theta =2\pi $ of the closed loop in the order parameter space, the $U(1)_{\mathrm {B}}$ contribution becomes $\omega = \exp (2\pi i /3)$. This factor is canceled by the $SU(3)_{\mathrm {C}}$ part ${\mathrm {diag}} (\omega ^2,\omega ^{-1},\omega ^{-1}) = \omega ^{-1} \textbf {1}_3$. Only the $U(1)_{\mathrm {B}}$ part with fractional winding cannot make a closed loop, while a contribution from the $SU(3)_{\mathrm {C}}$ part makes it possible to have a closed loop. One observes that the presence of the center group $\mathbb {Z}_3$ of the $SU(3)_{\mathrm {C}}$ group is essential. This is the same for the other two configurations.

Let us discuss the shape of the profile functions $f,g,h$. The corresponding Euler–Lagrange equations are given by
(3.34) \begin{equation} \left[\triangle - \frac{(2h+1)^2}{9r^2}-\frac{m_1^2}{6}\left(f^2+2g^2-3\right) - \frac{m_8^2}{3}(f^2-g^2)\right] f = 0,\label{eq3.34}\end{equation} 

(3.35) \begin{equation} \left[ \triangle - \frac{(h-1)^2}{9r^2}-\frac{m_1^2}{6}\left(f^2+2g^2-3\right) - \frac{m_8^2}{6}(f^2-g^2)\right] g = 0,\label{eq3.35} \end{equation} 

(3.36) \begin{equation} h'' - \frac{h'}{r} - \frac{m_{\mathrm{g}}^2}{3}\left(g^2(h-1) + f^2(2h+1)\right) = 0.\label{eq3.36} \end{equation} 

Here the masses $m_1$, $m_8$, and $m_{\mathrm {g}}$ are given in Eq. (2.28).

Before demonstrating the numerical solutions, let us examine the asymptotic behavior of the profile functions that can be analytically solvable. From these asymptotics, we find several peculiar properties of the non-Abelian vortices in the CFL phase. To this end, we consider small fluctuations around the asymptotic values $(f,g,h) = (1,1,0)$ and define
(3.37) \begin{equation}\label{eq3.37} \delta F(r) = (f(r)+2g(r)) - 3,\quad \delta G(r) = f(r)-g(r)-0,\quad \delta h(r) = h(r)-0. \end{equation} 

$\delta F(r)$ is the fluctuation of the trace part of $\Phi $ and $\delta G(r)$ is that for the traceless part proportional to $T_8$ as
(3.38) \begin{equation}\label{eq3.38} \Phi = \Delta_{\mathrm{CFL}} \textbf{1}_3 + \Delta_{\rm CFL} \left(\begin{matrix} \frac{1}{3} & & \\ & \frac{1}{3} & \\ & & \frac{1}{3} \end{matrix}\right) \delta F (x)+ \Delta_{\rm CFL} \left(\begin{matrix} \frac{2}{3} & & \\ & -\frac{1}{3} & \\ & & -\frac{1}{3} \end{matrix}\right) \delta G (r) + \cdots. \end{equation} 

The linearized field equations for the fluctuations are given by
(3.39) \begin{equation} \left(\triangle - m_1^2 - \frac{1}{9r^2}\right) \delta F = \frac{1}{3r^2},\label{eq3.39} \end{equation} 

(3.40) \begin{equation} \left(\triangle - m_8^2 - \frac{1}{9r^2}\right) \delta G = \frac{2}{3r^2}\delta h,\label{eq3.40}\end{equation} 

(3.41) \begin{equation} \delta h'' - \frac{\delta h'}{r} - m_{\rm g}^2\delta h = \frac{2}{3}m_{\rm g}^2 \delta G.\label{eq3.41} \end{equation} 

An approximate solution of the first equation is [112]
(3.42) \begin{equation}\label{eq3.42} \delta F = q_1 \sqrt{\frac{\pi}{2m_1 r}}\, e^{-m_1 r} - \frac{1}{3m_1^2 r^2} + O\left((m_1r)^{-4}\right). \end{equation} 

The first term is much smaller than the others, and is usually neglected, as in the case of the $U(1)$ global vortex. The dominant terms decrease polynomially, which is a common feature of global vortices. At high baryon density where $m_{\mathrm {g}} \gg m_{1,8}$, the solutions of Eqs. (3.40) and (3.41) are given by
(3.43) \begin{equation}\label{eq3.43} \delta G = q_8 \sqrt{\frac{\pi}{2m_8r}}\,e^{-m_8 r},\quad \delta h = - \frac{2}{3} \frac{m_{\rm g}^2}{m_{\rm g}^2 - m_8^2} \delta G. \end{equation} 

Here $q_{1,8}$ are some constants that should be determined numerically. This behavior of $\delta h$ is counterintuitive because the gluon has the magnetic mass $m_{\mathrm {g}}$ by the Higgs mechanism in the CFL phase, so naively one expects $\delta h \sim e^{-m_{\mathrm {g}} r}$. Since the asymptotic behaviors of the vortex string are deeply related to the intervortex forces, we expect that the intervortex forces of the non-Abelian vortices in CFL are quite different from those in conventional metallic superconductors.6 In the opposite case $m_{\mathrm {g}} \ll m_8$, the asymptotic behaviors are changed from Eq. (3.43) as
(3.44) \begin{equation}\label{eq3.44} \delta G = - \frac{2q_{\rm g}}{3} \frac{1}{(m_8^2-m_{\rm g}^2)r^2}\sqrt{\frac{\pi}{2m_8r}}\, e^{-m_8 r},\quad \delta h = q_{\rm g} \sqrt{\frac{\pi m_{\rm g} r}{2}}\, e^{-m_{\rm g}r}. \end{equation} 

In this case the asymptotic behaviors are governed by $m_{\mathrm {g}}$, which is smaller than $m_8$. The coefficients $q_8$ and $q_{\rm g}$ depend on the masses $m_1$, $m_8$, and $m_{\mathrm {g}}$ and can be determined numerically [112].

Let us compare the non-Abelian vortex explained in this section with the Abelian vortices in Sect. 3.1. The $U(1)_{\rm B}$ vortex is characterized by the first homotopy group $\pi _1[U(1)_{\mathrm {B}}]$. As can be seen from Eq. (3.4), the $U(1)_{\mathrm {B}}$ vortex has an integer winding number. Therefore, this vortex is called an integer vortex. The non-Abelian vortex is also characterized by $\pi _1[U(1)_{\mathrm {B}}]$, but its winding number takes the value of a fractional number. The minimal winding number is quantized by $1/3$. The vortices with fractional winding numbers are the so-called fractional vortices. To understand this, let us write the asymptotic behavior of $\Phi $ given in Eq. (3.33) at $r \to \infty $ as
(3.45) \begin{equation}\label{eq3.45} \Phi \sim \Delta_{\mathrm{CFL}} e^{\frac{i\theta}{3}} \textbf{1}_3. \end{equation} 

Here “$\sim $” stands for the equivalence under the $SU(3)_{\rm C}$ gauge transformation. This explicitly shows that the minimally winding non-Abelian vortex has only 1/3 winding number in the $U(1)_{\mathrm {B}}$ space. Consequently, the circulation of the superfluid velocity in Eq. (2.62) obeys a fractional Onsager–Feynman quantization
(3.46) \begin{equation}\label{eq3.46} c_B = \oint d x^i J^{\rm B}_i = \frac{2 \pi}{3} \frac{4K_3}{\gamma K_0} k \end{equation} 

with the vortex number $k \in \pi _1[U(3)_{{\mathrm {C}}-{\mathrm { F}}+{\rm B}}]$. This is 1/3 of the circulation (3.5) of a $U(1)_{\mathrm {B}}$ vortex.7

Finding the minimally winding solution is very important to find which configuration is the most stable in the CFL phase. Since the non-Abelian vortex is a global vortex, its tension consists of two parts: a logarithmically divergent part and a finite part. (3.47) \begin{equation}\label{eq3.47} \mathcal{T}_{{\mathrm{M}}_1}=\mathcal{T}_{{\rm div;M}_1} +\mathcal{T}_{{\rm fin;M}_1}. \end{equation}
A dominant contribution to the tension (logarithmically divergent) comes from the kinetic term as (3.48) \begin{equation}\label{eq3.48} \mathcal{T}_{{\rm div;M}_1} \simeq K_3\int d^2x\ {\rm Tr}\, \mathcal{D}_i\Phi (\mathcal{D}_i\Phi)^{\dagger} = \frac{1}{9} \times 6\pi \Delta_{\rm CFL}^2 K_3 \log \frac{L}{\xi}. \end{equation}
This should be compared with the tension of the $U(1)_{\mathrm { B}}$ integer vortex given in Eq. (3.8). Since the minimal winding number of the non-Abelian vortex is $1/3$, the tension of the non-Abelian vortex is $1/3^2 = 1/9$ times as large as that of the $U(1)_{\mathrm {B}}$ integer vortex. Thus, we conclude that the non-Abelian vortex is the most stable string configuration in the CFL phase. In Sect. 4.3, we discuss how a $U(1)_{\rm B}$ integer vortex decays into non-Abelian vortices. Fig. 3.
The non-Abelian vortex profile functions $\{f(r),g(r),h(r)\}=\{\text {green},\text {blue},\text {red}\}$. The mass parameters are set to be $\{m_{\mathrm {g}},m_1,m_8\} = \{5,1,1\}, \{1,5,1\}, \{1,1,5\}$. $g(r)$ is almost flat when $m_1 \simeq m_8$.
Fig. 4.
Distributions of the energy density (red) and the non-Abelian magnetic flux (blue) for the minimally winding non-Abelian vortex. The parameters are chosen to be $(m_{\rm g},m_1,m_8) = (10,2,1)$. While the energy density has a long tail, the magnetic flux whose transverse size is of order $m_8^{-1}$ exponentially converges to 0.

Let us next compare the color-magnetic fluxes of the non-Abelian vortices and non-topological color fluxes discussed in Sect. 3.2. As shown in Eq. (3.23), the flux of the 8th direction in the $SU(3)_c$ space of the Abelian vortex (in the limit of $\zeta = \pi /2$) is $4\sqrt {3}\pi /g_{\mathrm {s}}$. With the ansatz (3.26), the amount of flux of the non-Abelian vortex can be easily obtained as
(3.49) \begin{equation}\label{eq3.49} A_i = - \sqrt{\frac{2}{3}} \frac{\epsilon_{ij}x^j}{g_{\rm s}r^2}(1-h)T_8 \enspace \to \enspace \Phi_{\rm NA}^8 = \int d^2x~ F_{12}^8 = \frac{1}{3} \times \Phi_{\rm A}^8. \end{equation} 

Thus, with respect to the color-magnetic flux, the non-Abelian vortex is the minimal configuration in the CFL phase.

A similar but slightly different vortex configuration from the minimal (M$_1$) vortices, called M$_2$ vortices, was also found in Ref. [35]. The ansatz for the M$_2$ vortex is quite similar to that in Eqs. (3.25) and (3.26) as
(3.50) \begin{equation} \Phi(r,\theta) = \Delta_{\mathrm{CFL}} \left(\begin{matrix} q(r) & 0 & 0 \\ 0 & e^{i\theta}p(r) & 0 \\ 0 & 0 & e^{i\theta}p(r)\end{matrix}\right),\label{eq3.50} \end{equation} 

(3.51) \begin{equation} A_i(r,\theta) = -\frac{\epsilon_{ij}x^j}{g_{\rm s}r^2} \left(1-h(r)\right) \left(\begin{matrix} -\frac{2}{3} & 0 & 0\\ 0 & \frac{1}{3} & 0\\ 0 & 0 & \frac{1}{3}\end{matrix}\right).\label{eq3.51} \end{equation} 

The ansatz for $\Phi $ can be rewritten as
(3.52) \begin{equation}\label{eq3.52} \Phi(r,\theta) = \Delta_{\mathrm{CFL}} e^{i\frac{2\theta}{3}} \left(\begin{matrix} e^{-i\frac{2\theta}{3}} & 0 & 0 \\ 0 & e^{i\frac{\theta}{3}} & 0 \\ 0 & 0 & e^{i\frac{\theta}{3}} \end{matrix}\right) \left(\begin{matrix} q(r) & 0 & 0 \\ 0 & p(r) & 0 \\ 0 & 0 & p(r) \end{matrix}\right). \end{equation} 

This shows that an M$_2$ vortex has 2/3 winding in the $U(1)_{\rm B}$ space, which is twice as large as that of an M$_1$ vortex. For the rest, the ansatz goes into the $SU(3)_{\mathrm {C}}$ orbit; more explicitly, $S^1 \subset SU(3)_{\mathrm {C}}$, which is generated by $T_8$. In comparison with the M$_1$ vortex given in Eqs. (3.25) and (3.26), one finds that the circulation of the M$_2$ vortex goes in the opposite direction in the $SU(3)_{\mathrm {C}}$ orbit. Namely, the color of the M$_1$ vortex in Eq. (3.25) is $\bar r = gb$, while that of the M$_2$ vortex in Eq. (3.52) is $r = \overline {gb}$. Like the M$_1$ vortex, the tension of the M$_2$ vortex consists of a divergent part and a finite part. The divergent part is given by
(3.53) \begin{equation}\label{eq3.53} {\mathcal{T}}_{{\mathrm{div}};{\rm M}_2} = \frac{4}{9} \times 6\pi^2 \Delta_{\rm CFL}^2 K_3 \log \frac{L}{\xi}. \end{equation} 

This is four times as large as the divergent part of the tension of the M$_1$ vortex. This implies that an M$_2$ vortex with a red flux decays into two M$_1$ vortices with green and blue colors with opposite directions, as illustrated in Fig. 11(b).

Notice that an M$_1$ vortex with winding number 2 has the same divergent tension as $\mathcal {T}_{{\mathrm {div;M}}_2}$ because of
(3.54) \begin{equation}\label{eq3.54} \Phi(r,\theta) = \Delta_{\rm CFL} e^{i\frac{2\theta}{3}} \left(\begin{matrix} e^{i\frac{4\theta}{3}} & 0 & 0 \\ 0 & e^{-i\frac{2\theta}{3}} & 0 \\ 0 & 0 & e^{-i\frac{2\theta}{3}} \end{matrix}\right) \left(\begin{matrix} f_2(r) & 0 & 0 \\ 0 & g_2(r) & 0 \\ 0 & 0 & g_2(r) \end{matrix}\right). \end{equation} 

For the finite contribution of the color-magnetic flux to the energy, the M$_2$ vortex has lower cost since the M$_1$ vortex with winding number 2 has a color-magnetic flux twice as great as that of an M$_2$ vortex.

The vortices in the CFL phase are called non-Abelian vortices because they carry color magnetic fluxes, or equivalently the color gauge $SU(3)_{\mathrm {C}}$ transformation participate in the loop in the order parameter space. The three configurations in Eqs. (3.25)–(3.28) carry corresponding color magnetic fluxes. Since these configurations cannot be transformed into each other by the gauge transformation, these are all physically different vortices. However, note that the above three vortices do not exhaust all possible configurations. Indeed, there exists a continuous family of an infinite number of vortex configurations. To see it, let us decompose the ansatz Eqs. (3.25) and (3.26) as
(3.55) \begin{equation}\label{eq3.55} \Phi = \Delta_{\rm CFL} {\rm diag}(f e^{i\theta},g,g) \sim \Delta_{\rm CFL} e^{\frac{i\theta}{3}} \left(\frac{F}{3}\textbf{1}_3 + \sqrt{\frac{2}{3}}GT_8\right), \end{equation} 

where we have introduced
(3.56) \begin{equation}\label{eq3.56} F \equiv f + 2g, \quad G \equiv f - g \end{equation} 

and have transformed the configuration by a certain $SU(3)_{\rm C}$ gauge symmetry. Continuously degenerate configurations can be obtained by the color–flavor rotation as
(3.57) \begin{equation}\label{eq3.57} \Phi \to \Delta_{\mathrm{CFL}} e^{\frac{i\theta}{3}} \left(\frac{F}{3}\textbf{1}_3 + \sqrt{\frac{2}{3}}GU T_8 U^{\dagger} \right),\quad U \in SU(3)_{{\rm C}+{\rm L}+{\rm R}}. \end{equation} 

The three configurations in Eqs. (3.25)–(3.28) are particular diagonal configurations. It is now obvious that the color–flavor $SU(3)_{{\mathrm { C}}+{\mathrm { L}}+{\mathrm { R}}}$ symmetry of the ground state in the CFL phase is spontaneously broken in the presence of a non-Abelian vortex because of the term proportional to $T_8$ in Eq. (3.55). In the vortex core, there remains the unbroken symmetry
(3.58) \begin{equation}\label{eq3.58} K = U(1)_{{\mathrm{C}}+{\rm L}+{\rm R}} \times SU(2)_{{\rm C}+{\rm L}+{\rm R}} \end{equation} 

where $U(1)_{{\mathrm { C}}+{\mathrm { L}}+{\mathrm { R}}}$ is the Abelian subgroup generated by $T_8$ and $SU(2)_{{\mathrm {C}}+{\mathrm { L}}+{\mathrm { R}}}$ is a $SU(2)$ subgroup that commutes with $U(1)_{{\mathrm {C}}+{\mathrm { L}}+{\mathrm { R}}}$. Therefore, there appear, in the vicinity of the vortex, Nambu–Goldstone (NG) modes associated with this spontaneous symmetry breaking:
(3.59) \begin{equation}\label{eq3.59} \frac{H_{\rm CFL}}{K} =\frac{SU(3)_{{\rm C}+{\rm L}+{\rm R}}}{U(1)_{{\rm C}+{\rm L}+{\rm R}} \times SU(2)_{{\rm C}+{\rm L}+{\rm R}}} \simeq \mathbb{C}P^2. \end{equation} 

This space is known as the 2D complex projective space. These modes are called the orientational moduli (collective coordinates) of the non-Abelian vortex. The existence of these modes was first pointed out in Ref. [242] in the context of dense QCD, but it was known before in the context of supersymmetric QCD, as summarized in Appendix A. Points on the $\mathbb {C}P^2$ manifold correspond to the different fluxes. One can move from point to point by $SU(3)_{{\mathrm {C}}+{\mathrm { L}}+{\mathrm { R}}}$.

Let us next consider the orientation of the M$_2$ vortex. Going away from the M$_2$ vortex, one asymptotically reaches the ground state where the $SU(3)_{{\mathrm {C}}+{\mathrm { L}}+{\mathrm { R}}}$ color–flavor symmetry holds. On the other hand, since the condensation field $\Phi $ at the center of the M$_2$ vortex is
(3.60) \begin{equation}\label{eq3.60} \Phi \propto {\mathrm{diag}}(1,0,0), \end{equation} 

the color–flavor symmetry that does not change this is the same as that for the M1 vortex. Namely, $U(1)_{{\mathrm { C}}+{\mathrm { L}}+{\mathrm { R}}} \times SU(2)_{{\mathrm { C}}+{\mathrm { L}}+{\mathrm { R}}}$. There the M$_2$ vortex also has the orientational zero modes
(3.61) \begin{equation}\label{eq3.61} \frac{H_{\mathrm{CFL}}}{K} =\frac{SU(3)_{{\rm C}+{\rm L}+{\rm R}}}{U(1)_{{\rm C}+{\rm L}+{\rm R}} \times SU(2)_{{\rm C}+{\rm L}+{\rm R}}} \simeq \mathbb{C}P^2. \end{equation}

The existence of a vortex spontaneously breaks the translational symmetry. When a vortex is placed along the $z$-axis, it breaks two translational symmetries in the $x$–$y$ plane. When the vortex string fluctuates, the configuration can be written as
(4.1) \begin{align} \Phi &= f(\bar{r}) e^{i (\bar{\theta} + \alpha)}, \quad \bar{r} = \sqrt{(x - X)^2 + (y - Y)^2}, \\ \bar{\theta} &= \tan^{-1}\left(\frac{y - Y}{x - X}\right), \label{eq4.1} \end{align} 

where $X = X(t,z)$ and $Y = Y(t,z)$ denote the position of the vortex in the $x$–$y$ plane, which is a field in the vortex world-sheet, and $(\bar r, \bar \theta)$ are the polar coordinates from the vortex center. Inserting Eq. (4.1) into Eq. (2.55), and integrating the Lagrangian density over the $x$–$y$ plane, we obtain the effective theory [207]
(4.2) \begin{equation}\label{eq4.2} \mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{eff}} = - \mathcal{T} + 4 \pi \gamma K_0 (Y \partial_t X - X \partial_t Y) - \frac{\mathcal{T}}{2} [(\partial_z X)^2 +(\partial_z Y)^2], \end{equation} 

up to the quadratic order of $X$ and $Y$ and the leading order in derivatives. Here, $\mathcal {T}$ is the tension of the vortex, given in Eq. (3.8) for a $U(1)_{\mathrm {B}}$ vortex and Eq. (3.48) for a non-Abelian vortex. For a vortex in $d=2+1$, the second term can be found in Refs. [265,231]. The first and third terms in Eq. (4.2) are consistent with the spatial part of the Nambu–Goto action [245,153] at this order:
(4.3) \begin{equation}\label{eq4.3} \mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{NG}} = - \mathcal{T} \sqrt {1 - (\partial_z X)^2 - (\partial_z Y)^2}. \end{equation}

Let us remark on the effective Lagrangian in Eq. (4.2). Because the time derivative is at the first order in the Lagrangian, $X$ and $Y$ are not independent fields; rather, they are momentum conjugate to each other:
(4.4) \begin{equation}\label{eq4.4} P_X = \frac{\partial \mathcal{L}}{\partial (\partial_t X)} \sim Y, \quad P_Y = \frac{\partial \mathcal{L}}{\partial (\partial_t Y)} \sim X. \end{equation} 

Therefore, although two translational symmetries are broken, there appears only one independent gapless Nambu–Goldstone mode, known a Kelvin mode or Kelvon if quantized. There are two typical solutions of the effective Lagrangian (4.2):
(4.5) \begin{equation} X_1 = A \cos(k_z z - \omega t + \delta_1), \quad Y_1= A \sin(k_z z - \omega t + \delta_1), \label{eq4.5}\end{equation} 

(4.6) \begin{equation}X_2 = A \sin(k_z z + \omega t + \delta_2), \quad Y_2= A \cos(k_z z + \omega t + \delta_2), \label{eq4.6} \end{equation} 

where $\delta _1$ and $\delta _2$ are arbitrary constants. The first and second solutions show the clockwise and counterclockwise spiral Kelvin waves propagating along the vortex string in opposite directions, as illustrated in Fig. 6. The Kelvin waves have “chirality”: the (counter)clockwise waves can propagate only in one (the other) direction, which is well known in superfluids.
Fig. 6.

Kelvin waves. The Kelvin waves in Eqs. (4.5) and (4.6) propagate in definite directions indicated by the arrows.

Since $X$ and $Y$ are not independent but are momentum conjugate to each other, there is only one dispersion relation
(4.7) \begin{equation}\label{eq4.7} \omega = \frac{\mathcal{T}}{4 \pi \gamma K_0} k_z^2, \end{equation} 

which is quadratic. The Kelvon is an example of type-II Nambu–Goldstone modes.8

The two comments are addressed here. The translational modes are exact Nambu–Goldstone modes only for an infinite system size. For a finite system, a gap as a correction is present in the effective theory [207]:
(4.9) \begin{equation}\label{eq4.9} \mathcal{L}_{\rm gap} = \frac{\pi}{R^2} (X^2 + Y^2), \end{equation} 

which is “tachyonic.” However, this does not imply instability in non-relativistic cases. Instead, the chirality is broken because of this term: Kelvin waves with wavelengths longer than some critical length propagate in a direction opposite to that of modes with shorter lengths, which is contrary to conventional understanding.

Here, we restrict ourselves to $2+1$ dimensions. The translational zero modes $X$ and $Y$ are free fields in the leading order. Let us consider multiple vortices with the position $(X_i, Y_i)$ of the $i$th vortex. The effective Lagrangian of interacting vortices can be written as
(4.10) \begin{equation}\label{eq4.10} \mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{eff}} =\sum_i \left[-\mathcal{T}+ 4 \pi \gamma K_0 (Y_i \partial_t X_i - X_i \partial_t Y_i)\right]- E_{\rm int} (X_i,Y_i) \end{equation} 

where $E_{\mathrm { int}}$ is the interaction energy among vortices, which is calculated in the next subsection. The interaction of vortices with an external superfluid velocity can be introduced by the Galilei transformation
(4.11) \begin{equation}\label{eq4.11} (X',Y') = (X,Y) - (J^{{\mathrm{B}}, {\rm ext}}_x,J^{{\rm B}, {\rm ext}}_y)t \end{equation} 

with an external superfluid velocity $J^{{\mathrm { B}}, {\mathrm { ext}}}_{x,y}$:
(4.12) \begin{align} \mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{eff}} &=\sum_i \left[-\mathcal{T}+ 4 \pi \gamma K_0 (Y_i \partial_t X_i - X_i \partial_t Y_i)\right]\nonumber\\ &\quad- E_{\rm int} (X_i,Y_i)- 4 \pi \gamma K_0 \sum_i (Y_i J^{{\rm B}, {\rm ext}}_x -X_i J^{{\rm B}, {\rm ext}}_y). \label{eq4.12} \end{align} 

The equations of motion in the presence of contributions from other vortices and an external flow can be written as
(4.13) \begin{align} 4 \pi \gamma K_0 \frac{\partial X^i}{\partial t} &= + \frac{\partial E_{\rm int}}{\partial Y^i} + 4 \pi \gamma K_0 J^{{\rm B}, {\rm ext}}_x \nonumber\\ 4 \pi \gamma K_0 \frac{\partial Y^i}{\partial t} &= - \frac{\partial E_{\rm int}}{\partial X^i} + 4 \pi \gamma K_0 J^{{\rm B}, {\rm ext}}_y.\label{eq4.13} \end{align} 

The first terms on the right-hand sides are called the inertial force.

The dissipation is present at finite temperature as the term proportional to $K_{\mathrm { D}}$ in Eq. (2.57). It is known that the contributions from the dissipation can be taken into account in the equations of motion in Eq. (4.13) as [126,125,199] (4.14) \begin{align} 4 \pi \gamma K_0 \frac{\partial X^i}{\partial t} &= + \frac{\partial E_{\mathrm{int}}}{\partial Y^i}+ 4 \pi \gamma K_0 J^{{\rm B}, {\rm ext}}_x - K_{\rm D} \frac{\partial E_{\rm int}}{\partial X^i} - K_{\rm D} J^{{\rm B}, {\rm ext}}_y, \nonumber\\ 4 \pi \gamma K_0 \frac{\partial Y^i}{\partial t} &= - \frac{\partial E_{\rm int}}{\partial X^i} + 4 \pi \gamma K_0J^{{\rm B}, {\rm ext}}_y- K_{\rm D} \frac{\partial E_{\rm int}}{\partial Y^i} + K_{\rm D} J^{{\rm B}, {\rm ext}}_x.\label{eq4.14} \end{align}
The third and fourth terms on the right-hand sides are called the Magnus forces, both proportional to the coefficient $K_{\mathrm { D}}$ of the dissipation term in Eq. (2.57). In particular, the third term is a contribution from the other vortices and the fourth term is a contribution from the external flow. Note that the Magnus forces exist only when the dissipation $K_{\mathrm {D}}$ is present at finite temperature. When a vortex moves with velocity $v$, there is an external flow in the rest frame of the vortex. Therefore, the vortex feels the Magnus force from the external flow as illustrated in Fig. 7. The trajectories of the vortex and anti-vortex are bent into the directions of $f_{\rm Magnus}$, as a curve ball in fluid dynamics. Fig. 7.
Magnus force. $v$ is the velocity of a moving vortex or anti-vortex. $f_{\mathrm {Magnus}}$ is the Magnus force. The trajectories of the vortex and anti-vortex are bent into the direction of $f_{\mathrm {Magnus}}$, as a curve ball.

We first take one vortex as a reference $\Phi _0$,
(4.15) \begin{equation}\label{eq4.15} \Phi_0 = {\mathrm{diag}}(e^{i\theta}f,g, g). \end{equation} 

Then, the other vortex $\Phi $ with general orientation in ${\mathbb {C}}P^2$ relative to the reference vortex should be obtained by $SU(3)_{{\mathrm {C}}+{\mathrm { F}}}$ transformation to $\Phi _0$. When we consider two vortices only, only an $SU(2)_{{\mathrm {C}}+{\rm F}} \left (\subset SU(3)_{{\rm C}+{\rm F}} \right)$ rotation is enough to be considered for relative orientation to $\Phi _0$ without loss of generality:
(4.16) \begin{equation}\label{eq4.16} \Phi = \Phi_0 U_{\mathrm{F}} =\left(\begin{matrix} \left(\begin{matrix} e^{i\theta} f & 0 \\ 0 & g \\ \end{matrix} \right) u_{\rm F}^{-1} & 0 \\ 0 & g \end{matrix} \right) =\left(\begin{matrix} \left(\begin{matrix} e^{i\theta} a f & e^{i\theta} b f \\ -b^* g & a^* g \\ \end{matrix} \right) & 0 \\ 0 & g\\ \end{matrix} \right), \end{equation} 

where $u_{\rm F} \equiv \left (\begin {matrix} a^* & -b \\ b^* & a \\ \end {matrix} \right)$ (with $|a|^2 + |b|^2 = 1$) is an element of $SU(2)_{\mathrm {F}}$. This corresponds to a ${\mathbb {C}}P^1$ submanifold in the whole ${\mathbb {C}}P^2$. Any color gauge transformation keeps the physical situation unchanged if they are regular. Here we implement a twisted color transformation of $SU(2)_{\rm C}$, given by
(4.17) \begin{equation}\label{eq4.17} u_{\mathrm{C}}(\theta, r)= \left(\begin{matrix} a^* & -b e^{i \theta F(r)} \\ b^* e^{-i \theta F(r)} & a \end{matrix} \right) \end{equation} 

with $F(r)$ being an arbitrary regular function with boundary conditions $F(0)=0$ and $F(\infty)=1$. The former condition has been imposed to make the transformation regular at the center of the string. This is possible because $\pi _1 [SU(2)_{\mathrm {C}}] = 0$. The upper left $2 \times 2$ minor matrix of $\Phi $ in Eq. (4.16) is transformed to
(4.18) \begin{align}\label{eq4.18} u_{\mathrm{C}}(r,\theta) \left(\begin{matrix} e^{i\theta} a f & e^{i\theta} b f \\ -b^* g & a^* g \\ \end{matrix} \right) &=\left(\begin{matrix} |a|^2 f e^{i \theta}+|b|^2 g e^{i\theta F} & a^* b \left[-e^{i\theta F}+f e^{i\theta} \right] \\ a b^* \left[-1+f e^{i(1-F)\theta} \right] &|a|^2 g+|b|^2 f e^{i(1-F)\theta } \end{matrix} \right) \nonumber \\ &\simeq \left(\begin{matrix} e^{i \theta} & 0 \\ 0 & 1 \end{matrix} \right) \quad \hbox{for} \quad r \gg m_1^{-1},m_8^{-1},m_{\rm g}^{-1}. \end{align} 

This result means that $\Phi \simeq \Phi _0$ for the large distance $r \gg m_1^{-1},m_8^{-1},m_{\mathrm {g}}^{-1}$. Also, the fully opposite orientation can be obtained by another color gauge transformation,
(4.19) \begin{align}\label{eq4.19} &\left(\begin{matrix} e^{-i \theta F(r)} & 0 \\ 0 & e^{i \theta F(r)}\end{matrix}\right) u_{\rm C} \left(\begin{matrix} f e^{i \theta} & 0 \\ 0 & g \end{matrix}\right)u_{\rm F}^{-1} \\ &\quad \simeq \left(\begin{matrix} 1 & 0 \\ 0 & e^{i \theta} \end{matrix}\right) \quad \hbox{for} \quad {r \gg m_1^{-1},m_8^{-1},m_{\rm g}^{-1}}. \end{align} 

Note that one cannot change the topological number by use of this kind of regular gauge transformation. We have thus seen that spatial infinity of the string configurations is the same and does not depend on the orientational zero modes. This is just a consequence of the fact that the orientational zero modes are normalizable.

The total profile of the two string system is given by the Abrikosov ansatz:
(4.20) \begin{equation}\label{eq4.20} \Phi_{\mathrm{tot}}=\Phi_1\Phi_2, \quad A^{\theta}_{\rm tot}=A^{\theta}_1+A^{\theta}_2. \end{equation} 

The first ansatz does not depend on the ordering of the matrices because the second vortex transforms to diagonal at large distances, as shown in Sect. 3. $A^{\theta }_{1,2}$ is the gauge field configuration accompanied with the single string system of $\Phi _{1,2}$. For an anti-vortex, $A^{\theta }_{1,2}$ changes the signs.

In order to obtain the static force between them, we first calculate the interaction energy density of the two string system, which is obtained by subtracting two individual string energy densities from the total configuration energy density;
(4.21) \begin{align} \mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{int}}(r,\theta,R)&\simeq {\rm Tr} \left(|D \Phi_{\rm tot}|^2 -|D \Phi_1|^2 -|D \Phi_2|^2 \right) \nonumber \\ &= \pm \frac{2}{3} \left[\frac{-R^2 + r^2}{R^4 + r^4 -2 R^2 r^2 \cos (2 \theta)}\right],\label{eq4.21} \end{align} 

where we have neglected the potentials $V(\Phi _{\rm tot})=V(\Phi _1)=V(\Phi _2)=0$ and the field strength $F_{ij}^aF^{aij}=0$ at large distance [270]. Here and below, the upper (lower) sign indicates the quantity for the vortex–vortex (vortex–anti-vortex) configuration.

The tension, the energy of the string per unit length, is obtained for $R \ll L$ by integrating the energy density over the $x$–$y$ plane as
(4.22) \begin{align} E_{\mathrm{int}}(R,L) &= \pm \int _{0}^{L} dr \int _{0}^{2 \pi} d\theta r \mathcal{E}_{\rm int}(r,\theta,R) \\ &= \pm \frac{2 \pi}{3} \left[ -\log 4 -2 \log R + \log \left(R^2 + L^2 \right) \right],\label{eq4.22} \end{align} 

where the system size $L$ has been introduced as the IR cutoff. The force between the two vortices is obtained by differentiating $E$ by the interval:
(4.23) \begin{equation}\label{eq4.23} f(a,L) = {\mp} \frac{\partial E_{\mathrm{int}}}{2 \partial R} = \pm \frac{2 \pi}{3} \left(\frac{1}{R} - \frac{R}{R^2 + L^2} \right) \simeq \pm \frac{ 2 \pi }{3 R}, \end{equation} 

where the last expression is for $L \to \infty $. We can see that the force is repulsive (attractive) for the vortex–vortex (vortex–anti-vortex) configuration. The overall factors $1/3$ in Eqs. (4.21)–(4.23) are attributed to the fact that the tension of the fundamental non-Abelian vortex is reduced by $1/3$ compared to the usual Abelian vortex, then leading to $1/3$ erosion in the magnitude of the force.

We denote the positions of two vortices by $(X_1,Y_1)$ and $(X_2,Y_2)$. In the absence of the dissipation ($K_{\mathrm {D}}=0$) at zero temperature, the equations of motion contain only the inertial force terms on the right-hand sides of Eq. (4.14). Then, the two vortices separated by the interval $2R$ rotate around each other, as in Fig. 9(a): (4.24) \begin{equation}\label{eq4.24} X_1 + i Y_1 = - X_2 - iY_2 = R \exp (i t /R^2), \end{equation}
where we set the coefficients at one. On the other hand, a vortex and an anti-vortex move parallel to each other due to the inertial force, as in Fig. 9(b): (4.25) \begin{equation}\label{eq4.25} X_1 + i Y_1 = - X_2 + i Y_2 = R + i (1/R) t, \end{equation}
where we set the coefficients at one. The velocity of the pair is faster when the distance between them is smaller. Fig. 9.
Dynamics of two vortices. (a) Two vortices repelling each other rotate around each other by the inertial force represented by $f$ in Eq. (4.23). The velocity is faster as the radius of the circular orbit is smaller. (b) A vortex and an anti-vortex attracting each other move parallel by the inertial force. The velocity is faster as the distance between them is shorter.

The logarithmically divergent part of the tension of non-Abelian vortices is given in Eq. (3.48), while the tension of a $U(1)_{\mathrm {B}}$ integer vortex is given in Eq. (3.8). Therefore, the tension of a non-Abelian vortex is $1/3^2 = 1/9$ times as large as the tension of a $U(1)_{\mathrm {B}}$ vortex. Since the total energy of the constituent three non-Abelian vortices is $3 \times 1/9 = 1/3$ of that of the $U(1)_{\mathrm {B}}$ vortex when they are infinitely separated, we conclude that the $U(1)_{\mathrm {B}}$ vortex breaks up into three non-Abelian vortices with three different color fluxes, say red, blue, and green color fluxes, with total fluxes canceled out, as illustrated in Fig. 11(a): (4.26) \begin{align} \Phi &= \Delta_{\rm CFL} {\rm diag}\,(e^{i\theta},e^{i\theta},e^{i\theta}) \nonumber\\ &\to \Delta_{\rm CFL} {\rm diag}\,(e^{i\theta_1},1,1) \times {\rm diag}\,(1,e^{i\theta_2},1) \times {\rm diag}\,(1,1,e^{i\theta_3}),\label{eq4.26} \end{align}
where $\theta _{1,2,3}$ is the angle of polar coordinates at each vortex. In fact, in Sect. 4.2, we have seen that the interaction between two non-Abelian vortices is repulsive at large separation. There remains the possibility of metastability of the $U(1)_{\mathrm {B}}$ vortices, since each vortex in Eq. (4.26) must carry a color magnetic flux that contributes finite energy corrections to the tension. However, they decay with perturbations. After the decay, these three vortices rotate as in Fig. 9, because of the inertial force. Without dissipation at zero temperature, they rotate forever, while the radius increases in the presence of the dissipation at a finite temperature. Fig. 11.
Decays of a $U(1)_{\mathrm {B}}$ superfluid vortex and an M$_2$ vortex. (a) A global $U(1)_{\mathrm {B}}$ superfluid vortex decays into a set of three non-Abelian vortices with the total magnetic flux canceled out. (b) An M$_2$ vortex with a red magnetic flux decays into two M$_1$ vortices with green and blue magnetic fluxes directed in the opposite direction. The numbers represent the energy ratio when all of them are infinitely separated.

Next, let us show a detailed configuration of the decay process of a $U(1)_{\mathrm {B}}$ Abelian vortex in Fig. 12. The Abelian vortex initially located at the origin O decays into three non-Abelian vortices, denoted by the red, green, and blue dots, carrying corresponding color fluxes. The red, blue, and green non-Abelian vortices are encircled by the paths
(4.27) \begin{equation}\label{eq4.27} b_1 - r_3 + r_2, \quad b_2 - r_1 + r_3, \quad b_3 - r_2 + r_1 \end{equation} 

respectively. At the boundary of spatial infinity, the $U(1)_{\rm B}$ phase is rotated by $\exp [i \theta {\mathrm {diag}} (1,1,1)]$ with the angle $\theta $ of the polar coordinates from the origin O. Therefore, the $U(1)_{\mathrm {B}}$ phase is rotated by $2\pi /3$ along each of the paths $b_1$, $b_2$, and $b_3$. Let us suppose that the three paths in Eq. (4.27) enclose the three configurations in Eqs. (3.25), (3.27), and (3.29), respectively. Then, we find that the color gauge transformations $g(r) \in SU(3)_{\mathrm {C}}$ occur along the paths $r_1$, $r_2$, and $r_3$ as
(4.28) \begin{align} &r_1:\quad g(r)= \exp [i u(r) {\mathrm{diag}}(0,-1,1)] =\begin{cases} {\rm diag} (1,1,1), \quad r=0 \\ {\rm diag} (1,\omega^{-1},\omega), \quad r=\infty \end{cases}, \nonumber \\ &r_2:\quad g(r) = \exp [i u(r) {\rm diag}(1,0,-1)] =\begin{cases} {\rm diag} (1,1,1), \quad r=0 \\ {\rm diag} (\omega,1,\omega^{-1}), \quad r=\infty \end{cases}, \label{eq4.28}\\ &r_3:\quad g(r) = \exp [i u(r) {\rm diag}(-1,1,0)] =\begin{cases} {\rm diag} (1,1,1), \quad r=0 \\ {\rm diag} (\omega^{-1},\omega,1), \quad r=\infty \end{cases}, \nonumber \end{align} 

respectively, with a monotonically increasing function $u(r)$ with the boundary conditions $u(r=0)=0$ and $u(r=\infty)=2\pi /3$.9 We find that the origin O is consistently given by
(4.29) \begin{equation}\label{eq4.29} \Phi = \Delta_{\mathrm{CFL}} {\rm diag} (\omega^{-1},1,\omega). \end{equation} 

From a symmetry, permutations of each component are equally possible. The configuration of Fig. 12) is a $U(1)_{\rm B}$ vortex encircled by the path $b_1+b_2+b_3$ corresponding to unit circulation at large distance. However, at short distance, it is separated into a set of three non-Abelian vortices, each of which is encircled by the paths in Eq. (4.27). The paths $r_i$ contribute to color magnetic fluxes and each $b_i$ corresponds to 1/3 quantized circulation.
Fig. 12.

Detailed configurations of decays of a $U(1)_{\mathrm {B}}$ superfluid vortex. The $U(1)_{\mathrm {B}}$ vortex initially located at the origin O decays into three non-Abelian vortices, denoted by the red, green, and blue dots. $b_1$, $b_2$, and $b_3$ are paths with angles $2\pi /3$ at the boundary at spatial infinity, and $r_1$, $r_2$, $r_3$ denote the paths from the origin O to spatial infinities. For $b_1$, $b_2$, and $b_3$, the $U(1)_{\mathrm {A}}$ phase is rotated by $\exp [i \theta {\mathrm {diag}} (1,1,1)]$ with the angle $\theta $ of the polar coordinates.

The time evolution of a rotating system is generated by $H'$. Let us first recall the reason for this using a simple example, a non-relativistic point particle in a rotating frame. The Lagrangian of a point particle with mass $m$ is written as $L = m \boldsymbol{v}^2/2, $ where $\boldsymbol{v}$ is the velocity in the rest frame. The conjugate momentum and the Hamiltonian are given by
(4.30) \begin{equation}\label{eq4.30} \boldsymbol{p} = \frac{\partial L}{\partial \boldsymbol{v}} = m \boldsymbol{v}, \quad H = \boldsymbol{p} \cdot \boldsymbol{v} - L = \frac{m {\boldsymbol{v}}^2}{2}. \end{equation} 

Now let us move to the description in the rotating frame at an angular velocity $\boldsymbol {\Omega }$. Let $\boldsymbol{v}'$ be the velocity of the particle in the rotating system. The two velocities are related by
(4.31) \begin{equation}\label{eq4.31} \boldsymbol{v} = \boldsymbol{v}' + {\boldsymbol{\Omega}} \times \boldsymbol{r}. \end{equation} 

Since the Lagrangian mechanics is covariant under general coordinate transformation, we can switch to the rotating frame just by substituting $\boldsymbol{v}'$ into $\boldsymbol{v}$,
(4.32) \begin{equation}\label{eq4.32} L = \frac{m}{2} \left(\boldsymbol{v}' + \boldsymbol{\Omega} \times \boldsymbol{r}\right)^2. \end{equation} 

The conjugate momentum and Hamiltonian in the rotating system are given by
(4.33) \begin{equation}\label{eq4.33} \boldsymbol{p}' = \frac{\partial L}{\partial \boldsymbol{v}'} = m (\boldsymbol{v}' + \boldsymbol{\Omega} \times \boldsymbol{r}) \left(= \boldsymbol{p}\right),\quad H' =\boldsymbol{p}' \cdot \boldsymbol{v}' - L = \frac{\boldsymbol{p}'^2}{2m} - \boldsymbol{\Omega} \cdot \left(\boldsymbol{r} \times \boldsymbol{p}' \right), \end{equation} 

where we have used the cyclic property of the cross product, $\boldsymbol{p}' \cdot (\boldsymbol {\Omega } \times \boldsymbol{r}) = \boldsymbol {\Omega } \cdot (\boldsymbol{r} \times \boldsymbol{p}')$. Noting that $\boldsymbol{p}' = \boldsymbol{p}$, we can rewrite the Hamiltonian in the rotating frame as
(4.34) \begin{equation}\label{eq4.34} \begin{split} H'&= \frac{\boldsymbol{p}'^2}{2m} - \boldsymbol{\Omega} \cdot \left(\boldsymbol{r} \times \boldsymbol{p}'\right) \\ &= \frac{\boldsymbol{p}^2}{2m} - \boldsymbol{\Omega} \cdot \left(\boldsymbol{r} \times \boldsymbol{p}\right) \\ &= H - \boldsymbol{\Omega} \cdot \boldsymbol{L}, \end{split} \end{equation} 

which connects the Hamiltonians in the rest and rotating frames. The discussion above can be straightforwardly extended to many-body systems, as long as the interaction potential is invariant under rotation.

Now let us discuss the response of a superfluid to rotation. We consider the situation where a cylinder with radius $R$ is filled with a superfluid and there is one vortex in the center of the vessel. Then the superfluid velocity is given by $\boldsymbol{v} = \frac {n}{2 \pi r} \boldsymbol{e}_{\theta }$ with $n$ the winding number. The energy of a vortex per unit length is
(4.35) \begin{equation}\label{eq4.35} E = \int d^2x \frac{1}{2}\rho \, \boldsymbol{v}^2 = \frac{\rho n^2}{4 \pi} {\mathrm{log}} \left(\frac{R}{a}\right), \end{equation} 

where $\rho $ is the superfluid density and $a$ is the core radius ($R \gg a$). The angular velocity per unit length is written as
(4.36) \begin{equation}\label{eq4.36} L = \int d^2x \left(\boldsymbol{r} \times (\rho \boldsymbol{v}) \right)_z = \int_a^R 2 \pi r \cdot r \rho |\boldsymbol{v}| \simeq \frac{1}{2} n \rho R^2, \end{equation} 

where the term proportional to $a^2$ is neglected. Thus, the energy in the rotating system is given by
(4.37) \begin{equation}\label{eq4.37} E' =\frac{\rho n^2}{4 \pi} {\mathrm{log}} \left(\frac{R}{a}\right) - \frac{1}{2} \Omega n \rho R^2. \end{equation} 

If this energy is less than $0$, one vortex state is favored compared to the state without a vortex. We can define the critical angular velocity $\Omega _{\mathrm {c}}$,
(4.38) \begin{equation}\label{eq4.38} \Omega_{\rm c} \equiv \frac{n}{2 \pi R} {\rm log} \left(\frac{R}{a} \right). \end{equation} 

Thus, if $\Omega >\Omega _{\mathrm {c}}$, the state with the vortex is more favorable than the trivial state.

If one further increases the rotational speed, multiple vortices are generated along the rotational axis. They all have the same winding number, so the intervortex force is repulsive. All the vortices repel each other, resulting in the formation of a vortex lattice. In the end, the vortex lattice co-rotates with the vessel, which means that the superfluid velocity at the edge coincides with the speed of the wall. Then the circulation $\Gamma $ can be calculated as $2 \pi R \cdot R \Omega $. This should be equal to the sum of the circulations of all the vortices inside the vessel, $\Gamma = n N$, where $N$ is the total number of vortices. Therefore, the total number of vortices
(4.39) \begin{equation}\label{eq4.39} N = \frac{2S \Omega}{n}, \end{equation} 

where $S$ is the cross section of the vessel. In the case of an ordinary superfluid, the circulation of each vortex is equal to one, $n=1$.

Let us see the creation of vortices from another point of view. We here consider a BEC and denote the condensate by a complex scalar field $\Phi (x)$. In a rotating frame, the gradient term of the energy functional can be written as (see Eq. (4.34))
(4.40) \begin{equation}\label{eq4.40} E_{\mathrm{grad}} = \Phi^\ast \left(\frac{\hat{\textbf{p}}^2}{2m} - \boldsymbol{\Omega} \cdot \hat{\textbf{L}}\right)\Phi = \frac{1}{2m} |[\partial_i - im (\textbf{r} \times \boldsymbol{\Omega})_i]\Phi|^2 -\frac{1}{2}m r^2 \Omega^2 |\Phi|^2 \end{equation} 

where $\hat L_i = i \epsilon _{ijk}r_j \partial _k$ and $\hat p_i = \partial _i$, and the equality is meant up to a total derivative. The combination $D_i =\partial _i - i m(\textbf {r} \times \boldsymbol {\Omega })_i$ can be seen as a covariant derivative on the field $\Phi $. Then $A_i \equiv m \textbf {r} \times \boldsymbol {\Omega }$ can be seen as a gauge field and this system looks like a charged field under a constant magnetic field $2\boldsymbol {\Omega }$. Therefore, just like a type II superconductor under an external magnetic field, vortices come into a rotating superfluid. Let us make a comment on the trapping potential. One should add an external potential to trap the condensate, such as $V_{\mathrm {trap}} = \frac {1}{2} \omega ^2 r^2$ for BECs. This term and the last term in Eq. (4.40) can be combined as $ -\frac {1}{2} r^2 \Omega ^2 |\Phi |^2+ V_{\mathrm {trap}} = \frac {1}{2} (\omega ^2 - \Omega ^2) r^2 |\Phi |^2$. When the rotation speed $\Omega $ is less than $\omega $, the condensates can be trapped.

Then, what happens if one rotates CFL matter? We can repeat the energetical argument. When the vessel is large enough, the energy of a vortex is dominated by the superfluid velocity part and the contribution from the color flux is negligible, Thus, what is modified in the argument above is that $n$ should be equal to one third, $n = 1/3$. For realistic neutron stars, the number of vortices can be estimated as
(4.41) \begin{equation}\label{eq4.41} N_v \simeq 1.9 \times 10^{19} \left(\frac{1\,{\mathrm{ms}}}{P_{\rm rot}}\right) \left(\frac{\mu/3}{300 \,{\rm MeV}} \right) \left(\frac{R}{10 \,{\rm km} } \right)^2, \end{equation} 

where $P_{\mathrm { rot}}$ is the rotational period, $\mu $ is the baryon chemical potential, and the parameters are normalized by typical values. The corresponding intervortex spacing is given by
(4.42) \begin{equation}\label{eq4.42} \ell \equiv \left(\frac{\pi R^2}{N_v}\right)^{1/2} \simeq 4.0 \times 10^{-6} {\mathrm{m}} \left(\frac{ P_{\rm rot}}{ 1 \, {\rm ms }} \right)^{1/2} \left(\frac{300 \, {\rm MeV}}{\mu/3} \right)^{1/2}. \end{equation} 

Since the intervortex spacing is far larger than the size of a vortex core, which is given by inverse gluon/meson masses, gluons and mesons would not affect the force between two vortices. The intervortex force is dominated by the exchange of $U(1)_{\mathrm { B}}$ phonons. This justifies the treatment above, in which we have only considered the contribution of $U(1)_{\mathrm {B}}$ circulations.

A direct relation between a relativistic global vortex in a medium and a non-relativistic superfluid vortex was discussed in Refs. [223,85] and later also in the CFL phase [128]. When a relativistic global vortex is put into an uniform medium that violates the Lorentz invariance, it acquires a non-zero angular momentum and behaves as if it is a non-relativistic superfluid vortex [85]. To see this, let us consider the low energy effective theory for the $U(1)_{\mathrm {B}}$ Nambu–Goldstone mode at high density and zero temperature in Sect. 2.2.1,
(4.43) \begin{equation}\label{eq4.43} \mathcal{L} = f_{\rm B}^2 \left[(\partial_0\varphi_{\rm B} + 2\mu)^2 - v_{\rm B}^2 (\partial_i\varphi_{\rm B})^2\right]. \end{equation} 

Note that the time derivative $\partial _0\varphi _{\mathrm {B}}$ is replaced by $\partial _0\varphi _{\mathrm {B}} + 2\mu $ in order for the effective theory to have the same symmetry as the microscopic theory, QCD. Because of this peculiar dependence of $\mu $, the order parameter gets the following time rotation:
(4.44) \begin{equation}\label{eq4.44} e^{i\varphi_{\mathrm{B}}} \sim e^{-2i\mu t}. \end{equation} 

As a result, a global $U(1)_{\mathrm { B}}$ vortex acquires a non-zero angular momentum [128]. From the time dependence in Eq. (4.44), one can easily see that a relativistic global string in a medium has non-zero angular momentum [128]:
(4.45) \begin{equation}\label{eq4.45} M_{12} = K_0 \int d^2x \left(x_1 \partial_2\phi^*\partial_0\phi -x_2 \partial_1\phi^*\partial_0\phi \right) +{\mathrm{c.c.}} \sim k \mu K_0. \end{equation} 

Furthermore, by interpreting a dual of the Kalb–Ramond field strength $H^{\nu \lambda \rho }$ (completely antisymmetric tensor) as the superfluid velocity $\partial _{\mu } \varphi _{\mathrm {B}}$ [223,85],
(4.46) \begin{equation}\label{eq4.46} 2 \mu\, \delta_{0\kappa} = \partial_\kappa\theta_{\rm B} \sim \epsilon_{\kappa\nu\rho\lambda}H^{\nu\lambda\rho}, \end{equation} 

one can reproduce interaction similar to the inertial force of superfluid vortices, which is one of the peculiar properties of superfluid vortices; see Sect. 4.2.2. For instance, the stability of a moving vortex ring was shown in Refs. [85,83].

In the rest of this subsection, we omit all the massive modes and concentrate on the orientational zero modes of $\mathbb {C}P^2$. Let us first identify the orientational zero modes in the background fields $\Phi $ and $A_{\mu }$. We start with a particular non-Abelian vortex solution,
(5.1) \begin{align} \Phi^\star(x,y) &= \Delta_{\mathrm{CFL}} e^{\frac{i\theta}{3}} \left(\frac{F(r)}{3}\textbf{1}_3 + G(r)\sqrt{\frac{2}{3}}~T_8\right), \\ A_i^\star(x,y) &= - \frac{1}{g_{\rm s}}\frac{\epsilon_{ij}x^j}{r^2} h(r)\sqrt{\frac{2}{3}}~T_8.\label{eq5.1} \end{align} 

Then the general solution can be obtained by acting the color–flavor-locked symmetry on them as
(5.2) \begin{equation} \Phi(x,y) = U\Phi^\star U^{\dagger} = \Delta_{\rm CFL} e^{\frac{i\theta}{3}} \left(\frac{F(r)}{3}\textbf{1}_3 - G(r)\langle \phi\phi^{\dagger}\rangle \right),\label{eq5.2} \end{equation} 

(5.3) \begin{equation} A_i(x,y) = UA_i^\star U^{\dagger} = \frac{1}{g_{\rm s}}\frac{\epsilon_{ij}x^j}{r^2} h(r) \langle \phi\phi^{\dagger}\rangle.\label{eq5.3} \end{equation} 

Here $\langle \mathcal {O}\rangle $ is the traceless part of a square matrix $\mathcal {O}$ and we introduced a complex 3-component vector $\phi $ that satisfies the following relation:
(5.4) \begin{equation}\label{eq5.4} - \sqrt{\frac{2}{3}}~UT_8U^{\dagger} = \phi\phi^{\dagger} - \frac{1}{3}\textbf{1}_3 \equiv \langle \phi\phi^{\dagger}\rangle. \end{equation} 

Taking the trace of this definition gives a constraint
(5.5) \begin{equation} \phi^{\dagger} \phi = 1.\label{eq5.5} \end{equation} 

Furthermore, the phase of $\phi $ is redundant by definition. Thus, we find that $\phi $ represents the homogeneous coordinates of $\mathbb {C}P^2$.

Now we promote the moduli parameters $\phi $ to the fields depending on the coordinates $t,z$ of the vortex world-sheet by using the so-called moduli space approximation (first introduced by Manton for BPS monopoles [232,231]). Since we are interested in the low-energy dynamics of the orientational moduli fields, we restrict ourself to the study of the dynamics with a typical energy scale that is much lower than the mass scales of the original theory, namely $m_1$, $m_8$, and $m_{\mathrm {g}}$. Namely, we consider the situation where
(5.6) \begin{equation}\label{eq5.6} \left|\partial_{\alpha} \phi(z,t)\right| \ll \min\left\{m_1,m_8,m_{\rm g}\right\},\quad (\alpha = z,t). \end{equation} 

Omitting the higher derivative terms (massive modes) with respect to $z$ and $t$, the low-energy effective theory for the orientational moduli field can be obtained by plugging $\Phi (x,y;\phi (z,t))$ and $A_i(x,y;\phi (z,t))$ into the full Lagrangian (2.7). Note that $\Phi (x,y;\phi (z,t))$ and $A_i(x,y;\phi (z,t))$ depend on the full spacetime coordinates, which are obtained by replacing the moduli parameter $\phi $ with the moduli field $\phi (z,t)$ in Eq. (5.3). In order to construct the Lagrangian of the effective theory, we also have to determine the $x^{\alpha }$ dependence of the gauge fields $A_{\alpha }$, which are exactly zeros in the background solution. For this purpose, following Ref. [318], we make an ansatz
(5.7) \begin{equation}\label{eq5.7} A_{\alpha} = \frac{i\rho_{\alpha}(r)}{g_{\mathrm{s}}} \left[\langle \phi\phi^{\dagger}\rangle, \partial_{\alpha}\langle \phi\phi^{\dagger}\rangle \right], \end{equation} 

where $\rho _{\alpha }(r)$ ($\alpha =z,t$) are unknown functions of the radial coordinate $r$ at this stage.

Substituting all the fields $\Phi $ and $A_{\mu }$ into Eq. (2.7), we are led to the effective Lagrangian that consists of two parts,
(5.8) \begin{equation}\label{eq5.8} \mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{eff}} = \mathcal{L}_{\rm eff}^{(0)} + \mathcal{L}_{\rm eff}^{(3)}, \end{equation} 

with
(5.9) \begin{equation} \mathcal{L}_{\rm eff}^{(0)} = \int dxdy\ {\rm Tr}\, \left[ - \frac{\varepsilon_3}{2}F_{0i}F^{0i} + K_0 \mathcal{D}_0\Phi^{\dagger} \mathcal{D}^0\Phi\right],\label{eq5.9}\end{equation} 

(5.10) \begin{equation} \mathcal{L}_{\rm eff}^{(3)} = \int dxdy\ {\rm Tr}\, \left[ - \frac{1}{2\lambda_3}F_{3i}F^{3i} + K_3 \mathcal{D}_3\Phi^{\dagger} \mathcal{D}^3\Phi\right].\label{eq5.10} \end{equation} 

The Lagrangian of the ${\mathbb {C}}P^2$ model can be uniquely written up to the overall factors of the decay constants as
(5.11) \begin{equation} \mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{eff}}^{(\alpha)} = C^{\alpha} \mathcal{L}_{\mathbb{C}P^2}^{(\alpha)},\label{eq5.11}\end{equation} 

(5.12) \begin{equation}\mathcal{L}_{\mathbb{C}P^2}^{(\alpha)} = \partial^{\alpha} \phi^{\dagger} \partial_{\alpha} \phi + \left(\phi^{\dagger}\partial^{\alpha}\phi\right) \left(\phi^{\dagger}\partial_{\alpha}\phi\right),\label{eq5.12} \end{equation} 

where no summation is taken for $\alpha $. What we have to determine is the coefficient $C^{\alpha }$ in Eq. (5.11). After manipulating straightforward but complicated calculations given in Appendix C, we find that the coefficients are expressed as
(5.13) \begin{equation} C^0 =\frac{4\pi\alpha_3}{\lambda_3 g_{\mathrm{s}}^2} \int dr\frac{r}{2}\left[\rho_0'{}^2 + \frac{h^2}{r^2}(1-\rho_0)^2 + \frac{\beta_3m_{\rm g}^2}{\alpha_3}\left((1-\rho_0)(f-g)^2+\frac{f^2+g^2}{2}\rho_0^2\right)\right],\label{eq5.13}\end{equation} 

(5.14) \begin{equation} C^3 =\frac{4\pi}{\lambda_3 g_{\rm s}^2} \int dr\frac{r}{2}\left[\rho_3'{}^2 + \frac{h^2}{r^2}(1-\rho_3)^2 + m_{\rm g}^2\left((1-\rho_0)(f-g)^2+\frac{f^2+g^2}{2}\rho_3^2\right)\right],\label{eq5.14} \end{equation} 

with $\alpha _3 \equiv \varepsilon _3\lambda _3$ and $\beta _3 \equiv K_0/K_3$. The coefficients $C^{0,3}$ should be determined in such a way that the energy is minimized. To this end, we regard the coefficients as “Lagrangian” for the undetermined scalar fields $\rho _{\alpha }$. Namely, we should solve the Euler–Lagrange equations
(5.15) \begin{equation} \rho_0'' + \frac{\rho_0'}{r} + (1-\rho_0) \frac{h^2}{r^2} - \frac{\beta_3m_{\mathrm{g}}^2}{2\alpha_3}\left[(f^2+g^2)\rho_0 -(f-g)^2\right] =0, \label{eq5.15} \end{equation} 

(5.16) \begin{equation}\rho_3'' + \frac{\rho_3'}{r} + (1-\rho_3) \frac{h^2}{r^2} -\frac{m_{\rm g}^2}{2}\left[ (f^2+g^2)\rho_3 - (f-g)^2\right] =0,\label{eq5.16} \end{equation} 

with the boundary conditions
(5.17) \begin{equation}\label{eq5.17} \rho_{0,3}(0) = 1,\qquad \rho_{0,3}(\infty) = 0. \end{equation} 

Numerical solutions of $\rho _0(r)$, $\rho _3(r)$, and the Kähler class densities $c_0(r)$ and $c_3(r)$ ($C_i = (4\pi /g_{\rm s}^2)\int dr\, rc_i(r)$) with $\lambda _i = \varepsilon _i = 1$ ($i=0,3$) for the mass choices $\{m_1,m_8,m_{\mathrm {g}}\} = \{1,5,1\},$ $\{5,1,1\}, \{1,1,5\}$ are shown in Fig. 14. The corresponding background solutions are shown in Fig. 3.
Fig. 14.

The numerical solutions of $\rho _0(r)$ (blue) and $\rho _3(r)$ (purple) for $\{m_{\mathrm {g}},m_1,m_8\} = \{1,5,1\}$, $\{5,1,1\}$, $\{1,1,5\}$. The Kähler class densities $c_0(r)$ (blue) and $c_3(r)$ (purple) are also shown in the 2nd line.

Let us roughly estimate $C^{0,3}$. To this end, we first recall the boundary condition $f(\infty) = g(\infty) = 1$ and $h(\infty) = 0$. The profile functions $f(r)$, $g(r)$, and $h(r)$ approach the above boundary values at $r \sim \Delta _{\mathrm {CFL}}^{-1}$, which is the typical inverse mass of the background solution; see Eqs. (3.42) and (3.43). Reflecting this property, the function $\rho _{0,3}$ also approaches its boundary values $\rho _{0,3}(\infty) = 0$ around $r \sim \Delta _{\mathrm {CFL}}^{-1}$. Because of $\rho _{0,3}(0) = 1$, the values of the square brackets of the integrands of $C^{0,3}$ at the origin are $(f(0)^2+g(0)^2)\beta _3 m_{\mathrm {g}}^2/2\alpha _3 \sim \beta _3 m_{\rm g}^2/2\alpha _3$ and $(f(0)^2+g(0)^2) m_{\mathrm {g}}^2/2 \sim m_{\rm g}^2/2$, respectively. Therefore, one can estimate $C^{0,3}$ as
(5.18) \begin{equation}\label{eq5.18} C^0 \sim \frac{\beta_3 m_{\mathrm{g}}^2}{\lambda_3 g_{\rm s}^2 \Delta_{\rm CFL}^2} \sim \frac{\beta_3\mu^2}{\Delta_{\rm CFL}^2},\quad C^3 \sim \frac{m_{\rm g}^2}{\lambda_3 g_{\rm s}^2 \Delta_{\rm CFL}^2} \sim \frac{\mu^2}{\Delta_{\rm CFL}^2}. \end{equation} 

Note that $\lambda _3$ dependence disappears in the above expression. From this result, we can also estimate the velocity of the $\mathbb {C}P^2$ modes propagating along the vortex string as
(5.19) \begin{equation}\label{eq5.19} v^2 = \frac{C^3}{C^0} \sim \beta_3^{-1} = \frac{1}{3}. \end{equation} 

This should be taken with some care due to the uncertainty of the numerical solution; it is just a rough parametrical estimation. Indeed, since $C^{0,3}$ depends on the chemical potential $\mu $, $v^2$ should depend on $\mu $ as well. The precise $\mu $ dependence of the velocity of the $\mathbb {C}P^2$ mode has not yet been determined in the literature.

Let us now turn to find a contribution of the second term in Eq. (2.34) in the low-energy effective Lagrangian. By substituting Eq. (5.3) into the second term in Eq. (2.34), we get the following potential term in the $\mathbb {C}P^2$ nonlinear sigma model:
(5.20) \begin{equation} V_{\mathbb{C}P^2} = D \left(|\phi_3|^2 - |\phi_2|^2\right),\label{eq5.20}\end{equation} 

(5.21) \begin{equation} D\equiv \pi \varepsilon \Delta_\varepsilon^2 \int^\infty_0 dr r\left(g^2-f^2\right),\label{eq5.21} \end{equation} 

where $(\phi _1,\phi _2,\phi _3)$ is the homogeneous coordinate of $\mathbb {C}P^2$, which satisfies the constraint $|\phi _1|^2 + |\phi _2|^2 + |\phi _3|^2 = 1$. Note that $\Delta _\varepsilon $, $f$, and $g$ should be obtained after replacing $\alpha $ with $\alpha '$, as in Eq. (??). Note also that $D$ is positive and finite because $g-f$ is positive for everything over $r$ and gets exponentially smaller on going away from the vortex; see Eq. (3.43). In the limit $\varepsilon \to 0$, the low-energy effective theory is the massless $\mathbb {C}P^2$ nonlinear sigma model where all the points on $\mathbb {C}P^2$ are degenerate. This is because non-Abelian vortices with different orientations have the same tensions. Once non-zero $\varepsilon $ is turned on, almost all the points of $\mathbb {C}P^2$ are lifted and only one special point $(\phi _1,\phi _2,\phi _3) = (0,1,0)$ remains as the global minimum of the effective potential; see Fig. 15. This means that the non-Abelian vortex with the specific orientation with color “blue–red” and flavor “s–u” is energetically favored,
(5.22) \begin{equation}\label{eq5.22} \Phi_{su} \to {\mathrm{diag}}(\Delta_{ds},\ \Delta_{su} e^{i\theta},\ \Delta_{ud}),\qquad {\rm as}\quad r \to \infty. \end{equation} 

This matches the fact that the pairing gap $\Delta _{su}$ is smaller than $\Delta _{ds},\Delta _{ud}$ as in Eq. (2.33) so that the vortex whose string tension is proportional to $\Delta _{su}$ is more easily created than others. As shown below, the details of the dynamics suggest that all configurations except for the $(0,1,0)$ vortex are no longer stable, including the $(1,0,0)$ and $(0,0,1)$ vortices.
Fig. 15.

Contour plot of the effective potential for the $\mathbb {C}P^2$ modes in the $|\phi _1|^2$–$|\phi _2|^2$–$|\phi _3|^2$ space. The colors represent the values of the potential.

Let us estimate the lifetime of unstable vortices. For simplicity, we consider the decay from the $(1,0,0)$ vortex to the $(0,1,0)$ vortex (from the bottom-left vertex to the bottom-right vertex of the triangle in Fig. 15). Since the effective potential is lifted for $|\phi _3|\neq 0$ direction, it is reasonable to set $\phi _3=0$ from the beginning. Then we are left inside the $\mathbb {C}P^1$ submanifold in $\mathbb {C}P^2$. It is useful to introduce an inhomogeneous coordinate $u(t) \in \mathbb {C}$ by
(5.23) \begin{equation}\label{eq5.23} \phi_1 = \frac{1}{\sqrt{1+|u|^2}},\quad \phi_2 = \frac{u}{\sqrt{1+|u|^2}}. \end{equation} 

Then the low-energy effective Lagrangian can be rewritten as
(5.24) \begin{equation}\label{eq5.24} \mathcal{L}_{\mathbb{C}P^1} = C^0 \frac{|\dot u|^2}{(1+|u|^2)^2} + D \frac{|u|^2}{(1+|u|^2)}. \end{equation} 

The typical timescale appearing with this Lagrangian is
(5.25) \begin{equation}\label{eq5.25} \tau = \sqrt{\frac{C^0K_0}{D}}. \end{equation} 

In principle, we can numerically calculate $\tau $ for each $\mu $. Instead, here, we provide a simple analytical estimation. Since the profile function $f(r)$ for the winding pairing gap ($g$, $h$, and $\rho $) increases (decreases) with a typical scale $r \sim \Delta _{\varepsilon }^{-1}$ for $m_{\mathrm {g}} \gg m_{1,8}$ as shown in Eqs. (3.42) and (3.43), we find from Eq. (5.13)
(5.26) \begin{equation}\label{eq5.26} C^0 \sim \frac{m_{\rm g}^2}{g_{\rm s}^2\Delta_{\varepsilon}^2\lambda_3} \sim \left(\frac{\mu}{\Delta_{\varepsilon}}\right)^2 \frac{1}{\lambda_3}. \end{equation} 

Furthermore, $D$ is estimated from Eq. (5.21) as
(5.27) \begin{equation}\label{eq5.27} D \sim \varepsilon \sim m_{\mathrm{s}}^2\log\frac{\mu}{\Delta_{\varepsilon}}. \end{equation} 

Thus, the typical timescale of the rollover from the $(1,0,0)$ vortex to the $(0,1,0)$ vortex is estimated by
(5.28) \begin{equation}\label{eq5.28} \tau \sim \frac{1}{m_{\mathrm{s}}\sqrt{\lambda_3}}\left(\frac{\mu}{\Delta_{\varepsilon}}\right)^2 \left(\log\frac{\mu}{\Delta_{\varepsilon}}\right)^{\frac{1}{2}}. \end{equation} 

In the limit $m_{\mathrm { s}} \to 0$, $\tau \to \infty $ as anticipated.

Note that, since we are interested in a realistic situation like that inside the core of a neutron star, we have imposed $\beta $-equilibrium and electric charge neutrality conditions for deriving the $m_{\mathrm {s}}$-correction to the effective potential in Eq. (2.34). Let us see how the effective Lagrangian changes when we remove the conditions. The direct $m_{\mathrm {s}}$ correction was obtained in Refs. [178,179] as
(5.29) \begin{equation} \delta V = \frac{2}{3}\varepsilon\,{\rm Tr}\left[\Phi^{\dagger} \Phi\right] + \varepsilon\,{\rm Tr}\left[\Phi^{\dagger} X_8 \Phi\right], \label{eq5.29}\end{equation} 

(5.30) \begin{equation} X_8 = {\rm diag}\left(\frac{1}{3},\frac{1}{3},-\frac{2}{3}\right).\label{eq5.30} \end{equation} 

This leads to the effective potential on the $\mathbb {C}P^2$ manifold
(5.31) \begin{equation}\label{eq5.31} V_{\mathbb{C}P^2}' = \frac{2}{3}D \left(2|\phi_3|^2 - |\phi_1|^2 - |\phi_2|^2\right) = \frac{2}{3}D \left(3|\phi_3|^2 - 1\right). \end{equation} 

In this case the ground state of the potential is the $\mathbb {C}P^1$ submanifold defined by $|\phi _1|^2 + |\phi _2|^2 = 1$.

The $SU(3)_{\mathrm {F}}$ flavor symmetry is explicitly broken as in Eq. (2.52) by the electromagnetic coupling. Consequently, all non-Abelian vortices are not transformed by the $SU(3)_{{\rm C}+{\mathrm {F}}}$ action once the electromagnetic coupling is taken into account. Only the $SU(2)_{{\mathrm {C}}+{\mathrm { F}}} \times U(1)_{\rm EM}$ subgroup remains exact as in Eq. (2.54). In order to classify all possible configurations, we consider a closed loop in the order parameter space generated by the gauge/baryon group, which is given by the following transformation on the order parameter,
(5.32) \begin{equation}\label{eq5.32} \langle \Phi(\infty,\theta)\rangle = e^{i \theta_{\rm B}(\theta)} e^{i \gamma^{a}(\theta)T^{a}}\langle \Phi(\infty,0)\rangle \, e^{i \alpha(\theta) T^{\small{\rm EM}}} \end{equation} 

where $\theta $ ($0 \leq \theta < 2\pi $) is the angle coordinate of the space and $\theta _{\mathrm {B}}$, $\gamma ^{a}$, and $\alpha $ are monotonically increasing functions. Since $\Phi $ must be single valued, one obtains a relation at $\theta = 2\pi $:
(5.33) \begin{equation}\label{eq5.33} e^{i \gamma^{a}(2 \pi)T^{a}} =e^{-i \theta_{\mathrm{B}}(2 \pi)} e^{-i \alpha(2 \pi) T^{\small{\rm EM}}}. \end{equation} 

This is an equation for the possible symmetry transformations giving a closed loop, or, equivalently, a possible vortex configuration. Notice that the existence and stability of vortices related to various solutions of the above equation can in principle only be inferred by a direct study of equations of motion. It is possible to determine all the solutions of Eq. (5.33), e.g. using an explicit parametrization of the elements of $SU(3)$. In what follows we will analyze three types of solutions that cannot be related by $SU(2)$ color–flavor transformations: 1) “Balachandran–Digal–Matsuura (BDM)” case [35], 2) “$\mathbb {C}P^{1}$” case, and 3) “Pure color” case.

The first possibility is a closed loop generated by $T^{8}$ in $SU(3)$ and the electromagnetic $T^{\small{\rm EM}}$ alone:
(5.34) \begin{equation}\label{eq5.34} e^{i \gamma^{8}(\theta) T^{8}} =e^{-i \theta_{\mathrm{B}}(\theta)} e^{-i \,\alpha(\theta)T^{\small{\rm EM}}}. \end{equation} 

By putting $\theta = 2\pi $, we obtain the relation
(5.35) \begin{equation}\label{eq5.35} \frac{\gamma^{8}(2\pi)}{\sqrt6}+ \frac{\alpha(2\pi)}{3} =-\frac{2\pi}{3}, \quad \theta_{\mathrm{B}}(2\pi)=\frac{2\pi}{3}. \end{equation} 

The equation above determines the phases $\gamma ^{8}$ and $\alpha $ only up to a linear combination. This is a consequence of the fact that the two generators $T^{8}$ and $T^{\small{\rm EM}}$ are proportional and indistinguishable from each other on diagonal configurations. The configuration above is invariant under $SU(2)$ color–flavor transformations (singlet). Balachandran, Digal, and Matsuura considered this case only [35].

The second possibility is a closed loop generated by winding in the $SU(3)$ group around the $T^{3}$ direction too, in addition to $T^{8}$ and $T^{\small{\rm EM}}$:
(5.36) \begin{equation}\label{eq5.36} e^{i (\gamma^{3}(\theta) T^{3}+\gamma^{8}(\theta) T^{8})} =e^{-i \theta_{\mathrm{B}}(\theta)} e^{-i \,\alpha(\theta)T^{\small{\rm EM}}}. \end{equation} 

By putting $\theta = 2\pi $, we obtain the relation
(5.37) \begin{equation}\label{eq5.37} \frac{\gamma^{8}(2\pi)}{\sqrt6}+ \frac{\alpha(2\pi)}{3} = \frac{\pi}{3}, \quad\gamma^{3}(2\pi)=\pm {\sqrt2} \pi, \quad \theta_{\mathrm{B}}(2\pi)=\frac{\pi}{3}. \end{equation} 

The configuration is not preserved by color–flavor transformations, and we can generate a orientational moduli space using $SU(2)$ color–flavor transformations. In fact, we have the most general configuration of this type in the form:
(5.38) \begin{equation}\label{eq5.38} \gamma^{3}(2\pi)T^{3}\rightarrow \gamma^{b}(2\pi)T^{b}, \quad |\gamma^{b}|= {\sqrt2} \pi, \quad b=1,2,3, \end{equation} 

where $\rightarrow $ denotes a replacement; the $T^{a}$ above are the generators of $SU(3)$ that commute with $T^{8}$ and form an $SU(2)$ subgroup. Since the continuous family generated by the $SU(2)$ group is characterized by a $\mathbb {C}P^{1}$ submanifold inside the whole $\mathbb {C}P^{2}$ space, we call them “$\mathbb {C}P^{1}$” vortices.

In terms of the vector $ |\gamma ^{a}|$ introduced above, the two previous cases correspond to $ |\gamma ^{a}|=0$ and $ |\gamma ^{a}|={\sqrt 2}\pi $. These are the only two cases for which a nontrivial electromagnetic phase is allowed. In all the other cases, we must have:
(5.39) \begin{equation}\label{eq5.39} e^{i \gamma^{a}(\theta)T^{a}}=e^{-i\theta_{\mathrm{B}}(\theta)}. \end{equation} 

By putting $\theta = 2\pi $, we obtain the relation
(5.40) \begin{equation}\label{eq5.40} \theta_{\mathrm{B}}(2\pi) = \frac{2 \pi}{3},\quad \alpha(2\pi)=0. \end{equation} 

We call this the “pure color” case because it does not involve electromagnetic transformations, and is thus equivalent to the case of pure color vortices studied in the previous subsections without electromagnetic coupling. Notice that this case spans a whole $\mathbb {C}P^{2}$, while $SU(2)$ color–flavor transformations generate only $\mathbb {C}P^{1}$ orbits (apart from the case where only $\gamma ^{8}$ is non-zero, which is invariant).

The “BDM case” corresponds to the vortex studied by Balachandran, Digal, and Matsuura in Ref. [35]. Since we only need $T^{8}$ to generate the correct winding for this vortex, the idea is to restrict the action (2.7) to include only the gauge fields $A^{8}$ and $A^{\mathrm {EM}}$, by keeping all the other gauge fields to zero. Formally the action reduces to that of a $U(1)\times U(1)$ gauge theory, which we can then express in terms of the massless and massive combinations in Eqs. (2.47) and (2.48):
(5.41) \begin{equation}\label{eq5.41} SU(3)_{\mathrm{C}}\times U(1)_{\small{\rm EM}}\rightarrow U(1)_{8}\times U(1)_{\small{\rm EM}}\simeq U(1)_{0}\times U(1)_{\rm M}, \end{equation} 

where the arrow means that we truncate the model to its Abelian subalgebra. The Lagrangian reads
(5.42) \begin{align} \mathcal{L} &= {\rm Tr}\left[-\frac14\frac32 F^{0}_{ij}F^{0ij}\right] - {\rm Tr}\left[\frac14\frac32 F^{\rm M}_{ij}F^{{\rm M}ij}+ K_{1} \mathcal{D}_{i} \Phi^{\dagger}\mathcal{D}^{i} \Phi -\beta_{2}(\Phi^{\dagger}\Phi)^{2}+m^{2}\Phi^{\dagger}\Phi \right] \nonumber \\ &\quad -\beta_{1}({\rm Tr}[\Phi^{\dagger}\Phi])^{2}-\frac{3m^{4}}{4(3\beta_{1}+\beta_{2})}, \label{eq5.42} \end{align} 

with
(5.43) \begin{equation}\label{eq5.43} \mathcal{D}_{i}=\partial_{i}-ig_{\rm M}A^{\rm M}_i T^{\rm M}, \end{equation} 

and $g_{\mathrm { M}}$ and $T^{\mathrm {M}}$ in Eqs. (2.45) and (2.46), respectively. This form of the Lagrangian is applicable only for diagonal configurations for the order parameter $\Phi $. In the action above, the massless field decouples completely, as expected, while the massive field $A^{\rm M}_i$ couples to $\Phi $ in the standard way. The BDM vortex is constructed analogously to Sect. 3.3 with the only difference from the uncoupled case being the new coupling constant $g_{\mathrm {M}}$ instead of $g_{\mathrm {s}}$:
(5.44) \begin{align} & \Phi(r,\theta)_{\small{\rm BDM}}= \Delta_{\small{\rm CFI}} \begin{pmatrix} e^{i\theta}f(r) & 0 & 0 \\ 0 & g(r) & 0 \\ 0 & 0 & g(r) \end{pmatrix}, \nonumber \\ & A_{i}^{\rm M}T^{\rm M}= \frac1{g_{\rm M}} \frac{\epsilon_{ij} x^{j}}{r^{2}}[1-h(r)] \, T^{\rm M} , \quad A_{i}^{0}=0.\label{eq5.44} \end{align} 

As shown in Fig. 5, the tension of the vortex decreases monotonically with the gauge coupling. Since we have $g_{\rm M}>g_{\mathrm {s}}$, the BDM vortex has a smaller tension as compared to the corresponding vortex in the uncoupled case.

The $\mathbb {C}P^{1}$ case was considered for the first time in Ref. [356]. We have identified a vortex configuration that is a solution of the equations of motion that correspond to these new boundary conditions. To see this, we notice that, similarly to the previous case, we can consistently restrict the action to include only the gauge fields corresponding to generators commuting with $T^{8}$. This corresponds to formally reduce to the case
(5.45) \begin{align} &SU(3)_{\mathrm{C}}\times U(1)_{\small{\rm EM}} \rightarrow SU(2)_{\rm C}\times U(1)_{8}\times U(1)_{\small{\rm EM}} \\ &\quad \simeq SU(2)_{\rm C}\times U(1)_{0}\times U(1)_{\rm M},\label{eq5.45} \end{align} 

similarly to what we have done for the BDM case. The truncated Lagrangian now is as follows:
(5.46) \begin{align} \mathcal{L} &= {\rm Tr}\left[-\frac14\frac32 F^{0}_{ij}F^{0ij}\right] - {\rm Tr}\left[\frac14 \frac32 F^{\rm M}_{ij}F^{{\rm M}ij} -\frac14 F^{b}_{ij}F^{bij} +K_{1} \mathcal{D}_{i} \Phi^{\dagger}\mathcal{D}^{i} \Phi -\beta_{2}(\Phi^{\dagger}\Phi)^{2}+m^{2}\Phi^{\dagger}\Phi \right] \nonumber\\ &\quad -\beta_{1}({\rm Tr}[\Phi^{\dagger}\Phi])^{2} -\frac{3m^{4}}{4(3\beta_{1}+\beta_{2})}, \label{eq5.46} \end{align} 

where
(5.47) \begin{equation}\label{eq5.47} \mathcal{D}_{i}=\partial_{i}-ig_{\rm M}A^{\rm M}T^{\rm M}-i g_{\rm s}A^{b}T^{b}, \quad b=1,2,3; \quad [T^{b},T^{8}]=0. \end{equation} 

Here, the index $b$ is relative to the $SU(2)_{\mathrm {C}}$ factor. As before, the massless combination decouples completely, and we can set it to zero. Among ${\mathbb {C}}P^1$ vortices, diagonal configurations are ${\mathbb {C}}P^{1}_{+}$ and ${\mathbb {C}}P^{1}_{-}$ vortices given in the second and third configurations in Eqs. (3.27)–(3.30). One of the simplest vortex configurations of the type considered here has the following diagonal form:
(5.48) \begin{align} & \Phi(r,\theta)_{\mathbb{C}P^{1}_{+}}= \Delta_{\small{\rm CFI}} \left(\begin{matrix} g_{1}(r) & 0 & 0 \\ 0 & e^{i\theta}f(r) & 0 \\ 0 & 0 & g_{2}(r) \end{matrix} \right), \nonumber \\ & A_{i}^{\mathrm{M}}T^{\rm M}=-\frac{1}{2}\frac{1}{{g_{\rm M}}}\frac{\epsilon_{ij} x^{j}}{r^{2}}[1-h(r)] T^{\rm M}, \nonumber \\ & A_{i}^{3}T^{3}=\frac{1}{\sqrt2}\frac{1}{g_{\rm s}}\frac{\epsilon_{ij} x^{j}}{r^{2}}[1-l(r)]\,T^{3}. \label{eq5.48} \end{align} 

As explained in Eq. (5.38), we can generate a full $\mathbb {C}P^{1}$ of solutions by applying $SU(2)_{{\mathrm {C}}+{\rm F}}$ rotations to the configuration above. Once the ansatz (5.48) is inserted into the equations of motion, we obtain the equations given in the Appendix of Ref. [356]. We have numerically solved these equations, and determined the energy of the vortex configuration. The tension, as schematically shown in Fig. 16, is higher for the $\mathbb {C}P^{1}$ vortices than for the BDM vortex. This result can be intuitively understood if we recall the observation of the previous section, where we noticed that the tension of a color vortex decreases monotonically with the gauge coupling. The $\mathbb {C}P^{1}$ vortex is built with both $g_{\mathrm {s}}$ and $g_{\mathrm {M}}$ couplings; thus, the interactions depending on $g_{\mathrm {s}}<g_{\mathrm { M}}$ contribute to increase the tension with respect to a vortex built exclusively from $g_{\mathrm {M}}$.
Fig. 16.

The three different special vortex solutions. The BDM vortex sits in the bottom-left corner of the toric diagram and has the lowest tension. The other two diagonal vortices are located at the remaining corners and are connected by color–flavor transformations. All of the upper right edge of the diagram represents the whole family of vortex configurations related by this $SU(2)_{{\mathrm {C}}+{\mathrm { F}}}$ transformations, namely a $\mathbb {C}P^{1}$ moduli space. These vortices have a slightly larger tension compared to the BDM configuration. The dashed line in the middle of the diagram corresponds to vortices not winding along the $T^{8}$ direction in the color group. These vortices have the largest tension and do not involve the electromagnetic gauge field.

Let us now come to the third case, the “pure color” case. This situation is realized when the boundary conditions are the same as the case without electromagnetic coupling. This means that, at infinity, only the non-Abelian gauge fields give nontrivial winding, and thus non-vanishing fluxes, while the electromagnetic gauge field is zero everywhere. However, this situation is generically not compatible with the coupled equations of motion. An easy way to see this is the fact that boundary conditions in the pure color case imply a non-zero flux for the massless field $A_{i}^{0}$. Since $A_{i}^{0}$ is massless and unbroken, there is no topology, compatible with the equations of motion, stabilizing and confining the flux. We thus expect that no solutions exist in general for the pure case. However, a special configuration that we call the “pure color vortex” is an exception to this statement. This configuration is defined as that corresponding to the following boundary conditions:
(5.49) \begin{equation}\label{eq5.49} e^{i \gamma^{a}(2\pi)T^{a}}=e^{-i\theta_{\mathrm{B}}(2\pi)}; \quad \gamma^{8}\equiv 0. \end{equation} 

Notice that the conditions above are perfectly consistent. We can take any configuration of $\gamma ^{a}$ phases and set $\gamma ^{8}$ to zero using a gauge(-flavor) transformation. This means that we can consistently set:
(5.50) \begin{equation}\label{eq5.50} A_{i}^{8}=A_{i}^{\mathrm{EM}}=0, \quad \Rightarrow \quad A_{i}^{\rm M}=A_{i}^{0}=0. \end{equation} 

In fact, $A_{i}^{\rm EM}=0$ means that for the pure color vortex we can consistently restrict the action by simply dropping all the terms involving $A_{i}^{\mathrm {EM}}$. As a consequence, the pure color vortex is exactly the same configuration as we get in the uncoupled case. Moreover, it satisfies the full equations of motion of the coupled case, because of the consistent restriction. Notice that the dependence of the electromagnetic gauge coupling also disappears completely from the restricted action. This means that the tension of the pure color vortex is also the same as the tension of the uncoupled vortices, involving only $g_{\mathrm {s}}$. As represented in Fig. 16, the tension of this vortex is larger than both the BDM and the $\mathbb {C}P^{1}$ vortices.

Because of this very fact we are led to conclude that the pure vortex is in fact a stationary point for the energy functional, but it corresponds to the global maximum. Both the BDM and the $\mathbb {C}P^{1}$ vortices are, on the other hand, local minima, with the BDM vortex being the absolute minimum. The $\mathbb {C}P^{1}$ vortices are, however, metastable, and if long-lived can play a crucial role in the physics of the CFL superconducting phase together with the BDM vortex. The whole situation is schematically summarized in Fig. 16. The numerical values of the tensions are compared as
(5.51) \begin{equation}\label{eq5.51} \mathcal{T}_{\text{pure}}-\mathcal{T}_{\small{\rm BDM}}=0.0176 \,{\mathrm{MeV}}^{2}, \quad \mathcal{T}_{\text{pure}}- \mathcal{T}_{\mathbb{C}P^{1}}= 0.0044 \,{\rm MeV}^{2}, \end{equation} 

for the same “realistic” choice of parameters made in Fig. 5, where in addition we have chosen $m_{\mathrm { g}}=92$ MeV and a value for the electromagnetic coupling constant of $e^{2}=1/137$. Notice that the expressions shown above do not depend on the infrared regulator $L$. The diverging parts of the tensions in Eq. (3.48) are equal and cancel out in the differences.

The BDM vortex carries a quantized $A_{i}^{\mathrm {M}}$ flux:
(5.52) \begin{equation}\label{eq5.52} \Phi^{\rm M}_{\small{\rm BDM}} = \oint \vec A^{\rm M}\cdot d\vec l=\frac{2 \pi}{g_{\rm M}}. \end{equation} 

Because of the mixing in the ground state, this gives the following non-quantized fluxes for the color and electromagnetic fields:
(5.53) \begin{equation}\label{eq5.53} \Phi^{8}_{\small{\rm BDM}}=\sqrt\frac{2}{3}\frac{1}{1+\delta^{2}}\frac{2 \pi}{g_{\rm s}},\quad \Phi^{\small{\rm EM}}_{\small{\rm BDM}}=\frac{\delta^{2}}{1+\delta^{2}} \frac{2 \pi}{e}, \quad \delta^{2} \equiv \frac23 \frac{e^{2}}{g_{\rm s}^{2}}. \end{equation} 

The fluxes of the $\mathbb {C}P^{1}$ vortices can be similarly determined; see Fig. 17:
(5.54) \begin{align} \Phi^{\mathrm{M}}_{\mathbb{C}P^{1}} &=-\frac{1}{2} \Phi^{\rm M}_{\small{\rm BDM}} \Rightarrow \Phi^{8}_{\mathbb{C}P^{1}}=-\frac{1}{2} \Phi^{8}_{\small{\rm BDM}},\quad \Phi^{\small{\rm EM}}_{\mathbb{C}P^{1}}=-\frac{1}{2} \Phi^{\small{\rm EM}}_{\small{\rm BDM}}, \nonumber \\ \Phi^{3}_{\mathbb{C}P^{1}_{+}} &= \oint \vec A^{3}\cdot d\vec l =\frac{1}{\sqrt2}\frac{2 \pi}{g_{\rm s}},\quad \Phi^{3}_{\mathbb{C}P^{1}_{-}}=-\Phi^{3}_{\mathbb{C}P^{1}_{+}}.\label{eq5.54} \end{align} 

Moreover, the quantized circulations of the BDM and $\mathbb {C}P^{1}$ vortices are the same as that of the usual uncoupled vortices, and are equal to $c_{B}\sim 1/3$ in Eq. (3.46).
Fig. 17.

The three different types of vortices we consider. The BDM vortex is labeled as the anti-red $\bar {r}$ vortex, as explained in the text, and carries twice the electromagnetic flux of the other solutions, the anti-blue $\bar {b}$ and anti-green $\bar {g}$ vortices.

We now apply the formula above to our specific case. The mass of the kinks can be estimated as being of the order of the square root of the height of the potential times the “size” $\beta $ of the moduli space:
(5.56) \begin{equation}\label{eq5.56} M_{\mathrm{kink}}\sim \beta \sqrt{\mathcal{T}_{\text{pure}}-\mathcal{T}_{\mathbb{C}P^{1}}}. \end{equation} 

The quantity $\beta $ has been evaluated analytically and numerically in Refs. [116,150]:
(5.57) \begin{equation}\label{eq5.57} \beta \sim K_{1}^{2}/\beta_{1}\sim \mu^{2}/T_{\mathrm{c}}^{2}, \end{equation} 

and it turns out to be large for our “realistic” regime, $\beta \sim 2500$. The tension difference is given by $\Delta \mathcal {T}=\mathcal {T}_{\text {pure}}-\mathcal {T}_{\small{\rm BDM}}$. We have already reported numerical estimates of the quantities above in Eq. (5.51), for a special value of the couplings. The decay probability is then:
(5.58) \begin{equation}\label{eq5.58} P\sim e^{-\beta\,R},\quad R\equiv\frac{\mathcal{T}_{\text{pure}} -\mathcal{T}_{\mathbb{C}P^{1}}}{\mathcal{T}_{\text{pure}}-\mathcal{T}_{\small{\rm BDM}}}. \end{equation}

Let us first rescale the variables as
(5.59) \begin{equation}\label{eq5.59} t \to \sqrt{C^0} t,\quad z \to \sqrt{C^3} z,\quad \phi \to \sqrt{C^0C^3} \phi. \end{equation} 

Then the low energy effective Lagrangian given in Eqs. (5.8)–(5.12) can be rewritten in the following form:
(5.60) \begin{equation}\label{eq5.60} S = \int d^2x\ \left[\left|(\partial_{\alpha} - iA_{\alpha}) \phi \right|^2 - \sigma \left(|\phi|^2 - \frac{N_{\rm C}}{3}\sqrt{C^0C^3}\right)\right]. \end{equation} 

Here we have performed the Hubbard–Stratonovich transformation by introducing the auxiliary gauge field $A_{\alpha }$ and also the Lagrange multiplier $\sigma $. After the rescaling, the partition function has the standard normalization as
(5.61) \begin{equation}\label{eq5.61} Z = \int \left[d\phi d\phi^{\dagger} d\sigma dA_{\alpha}\right]e^{iS}. \end{equation} 

Integrating out $\phi $ and $\phi ^{\dagger }$, one obtains
(5.62) \begin{equation}\label{eq5.62} Z = \int\left[d\sigma A_{\alpha}\right] \exp \left\{ - N_{\mathrm{C}} {\rm Tr}\, \log\left[ - \left(\partial_{\alpha} + i A_{\alpha}\right)^2 - \sigma \right] + \frac{iN_{\rm C}}{3}\sqrt{C^0C^3}\int d^2x~\sigma\right\}. \end{equation} 

Following [370], we solve this in a stationary phase approximation. From the “Lorentz” symmetry, the saddle point should be a point with $A_{\alpha } = 0$ and $\sigma =$ constant. Then, we vary the partition function in terms of $\sigma $ leading to the following gap equation:
(5.63) \begin{equation}\label{eq5.63} i\frac{\sqrt{C^0C^3}}{3}+ \int^{\Lambda = \Delta_{\mathrm{CFL}}} \frac{d^2k}{(2\pi)^2} \frac{1}{k^2-\sigma + i\epsilon} = 0. \end{equation} 

Note here that we have introduced the cutoff scale $\Lambda = \Delta _{\mathrm { CFL}}$, which is not a dynamical cutoff of the $\mathbb {C}P^2$ model but is a physical cutoff $\Delta _{\mathrm {CFL}}$ below which the description in the low energy effective theory does make sense. After integration, we get
(5.64) \begin{equation}\label{eq5.64} M^2 \equiv \sigma = \Delta_{\rm CFL}^2 e^{-4\pi \frac{\sqrt{C^0C^3}}{3}} \sim \Delta_{\rm CFL}^2 e^{-\gamma \left(\frac{\mu}{\Delta_{\rm CFL}}\right)^2} >0, \end{equation} 

where $\gamma $ is a certain positive constant and we have used $C^0 \sim C^3 \sim (\mu /\Delta _{\mathrm {CFL}})^2$ under the assumption $\lambda _3 \sim \varepsilon _3 \sim 1$. Looking back to the original action Eq. (5.60), one sees that a positive expectation value of $\sigma $ is a mass of $\phi $ and $\phi ^{\dagger }$.

Let us next consider fluctuations of $\sigma $ and $A_{\alpha }$ around the saddle point. It was shown [370] that higher order terms in $\sigma $ and $A_{\alpha }$ are suppressed in the large-$N_{\mathrm {C}}$ limit; only the quadratic terms are relevant. It turns out that the relevant Feynman diagrams are the propagator of $A_{\alpha }$ at the one-loop level [370]. Thus the effective world-sheet theory including the quantum effects to leading order of $1/N_{\mathrm {C}}$ is
(5.65) \begin{equation}\label{eq5.65} \mathcal{L}_{\rm eff}^{\rm quant} = \left|(\partial_{\alpha} - iA_{\alpha})\phi\right|^2 - M^2 |\phi|^2 - \frac{N_{\rm C}}{48\pi M^2}F_{\alpha\beta}^2. \end{equation} 

By rescaling $A_{\alpha }$ so that the kinetic terms of $A_{\alpha }$ are canonically normalized,
(5.66) \begin{equation}\label{eq5.66} A_{\alpha} \to \sqrt{\frac{12\pi M^2}{N_{\mathrm{C}}}}\,A_{\alpha}, \end{equation} 

the final form of the quantum effective theory reads
(5.67) \begin{equation}\label{eq5.67} \mathcal{L}_{\rm eff}^{\rm quant} = \left|(\partial_{\alpha} - ieA_{\alpha})\phi\right|^2 - M^2 |\phi|^2 - \frac{1}{4}F_{\alpha\beta}^2, \quad e\equiv \sqrt{\frac{12\pi}{N_{\rm C}}}\,M. \end{equation} 

In summary, taking the quantum effects into account, the auxiliary gauge fields become dynamical and $\phi $ acquires the $U(1)$ charge $e$ and a non-zero mass, which is consistent with the Coleman–Mermin–Wagner theorem in $1+1$ dimensions [78,239].

We are now ready to understand the above quantum phenomena in $1+1$ dimensions from the $3+1$ dimensional viewpoint. A clue is that the particles $\phi $ and $\phi ^{\dagger }$ in $1+1$ dimensions can be interpreted as topological solitons, namely a kink and an anti-kink, as was found by Witten [370]. This might be best seen in a supersymmetric $\mathbb {C}P^{N_{\mathrm {C}}-1}$ model, which has $N_{\mathrm {C}}$ degenerate quantum vacua with $\mathbb {Z}_{N_{\mathrm {C}}}$ symmetry. The kinks interpolate two adjacent vacua among the $N_{\mathrm {C}}$ vacua. Remember that, for us, the $\mathbb {C}P^{N_{\mathrm {C}}-1}$ fields $\phi $ and $\phi ^{\dagger }$ are the internal orientational moduli of the non-Abelian vortex in the CFL phase. Therefore, the kink on the vortex is a particle soliton in $3+1$ dimensions and is a junction of two different non-Abelian vortices. Thereby, it is nothing but a magnetic monopole in the Higgs phase; see Fig. 19. The mass of the monopole can be read from Eq. (5.65) as (5.68) \begin{equation}\label{eq5.68} M_{\mathrm{monopole}} = M \sim \Delta_{\rm CFL} e^{-\frac{\gamma}{2}\left(\frac{\mu}{\Delta_{\rm CFL}}\right)^2}. \end{equation}
This is much smaller than the mass $\sim \Delta _{\rm CFL} \log (L/\xi)$ of the non-Abelian vortex of the unit length. Dealing with the monopole as the soliton in the low energy world-sheet theory is justified by this hierarchy in the mass scales. Fig. 19.
A monopole as a kink of the orientational moduli $\phi $ in supersymmetric models.

Though the kinks on the vortex string are deconfined in the supersymmetric $\mathbb {C}P^{N_{\mathrm {C}}-1}$ model [160,149], they are confined in the non-supersymmetric case. This is because the particles $\phi $ and $\phi ^{\dagger }$ have charges $\pm e$ for the dynamical $U(1)$ gauge field as in Eq. (5.67). In one space dimension the Coulomb potential is linear, so that, even if the charges are small, they are confined. This is the reason why Witten referred to $\phi $ and $\phi ^{\dagger }$ as “quarks” (for us they are magnetic monopoles). The linear potential between a monopole at $z=z_1$ and an anti-monopole at $z=z_2$ is given by
(5.69) \begin{equation}\label{eq5.69} V_{\mathrm{conf}} = e^2|z_1 - z_2|. \end{equation} 

We illustrate the situation where the monopole and the anti-monopole are confined on the non-Abelian vortex in Fig. 20. The monopole and the anti-monopole are accompanied by semi-infinite long strings (vortex 1 in Fig. 20). The tension of vortex 1 is given by
(5.70) \begin{equation}\label{eq5.70} T_1 = T_{\rm classical} - \frac{N_{\rm C}}{3}M^2 \sim T_{\rm classical} - \frac{N_{\rm C}}{3}\Delta_{\rm CFL}^2 e^{-\gamma\left(\frac{\mu}{\Delta_{\rm CFL}}\right)^2}. \end{equation} 

From Eq. (5.69) we see that the vortex string (vortex 2 in Fig. 20) has a tension $T_2$ bigger than that of vortex 1 by
(5.71) \begin{equation}\label{eq5.71} T_2 - T_1 = e^2 = \frac{12\pi}{N_{\mathrm{C}}}M^2 \sim \frac{12\pi}{N_{\rm C}} \Delta_{\rm CFL}^2 e^{-\gamma\left(\frac{\mu}{\Delta_{\rm CFL}}\right)^2}. \end{equation} 

Fig. 20.

A confined monopole and anti-monopole in non-supersymmetric models.

There is another perspective for understanding this phenomenon [149]. The vacuum structure of the non-supersymmetric $\mathbb {C}P^{N_{\rm C}-1}$ model can be realized by looking at the $\vartheta $ dependence of the theory [373,322,149] by adding the $\vartheta $ term
(5.72) \begin{equation}\label{eq5.72} \mathcal{L}_{\vartheta} = \frac{\vartheta}{2\pi} \epsilon^{\alpha\beta}\partial_{\beta} A_{\alpha}, \end{equation} 

where $A_{\alpha }$ is that before rescaling (5.66). Recalling that the vacuum energy $E(\vartheta)$ is of order $N_{\mathrm {C}}$ in the large $N_{\mathrm {C}}$ limit, $E(\vartheta)$ is expressed as [373,149]
(5.73) \begin{equation}\label{eq5.73} E(\vartheta) = N_{\mathrm{C}} M^2 f\left(\frac{\vartheta}{N_{\rm C}}\right). \end{equation} 

Here $f(\vartheta)$ is an even function of $\vartheta $ due to the CP symmetry under which $\vartheta $ transforms as $\vartheta \to -\vartheta $. Furthermore, $E(\vartheta)$ is a periodic function as
(5.74) \begin{equation}\label{eq5.74} E(\vartheta + 2\pi) = E(\vartheta). \end{equation} 

One might suspect that these two conditions are incompatible at first sight. However, there is a way out: both of them can be satisfied when $E(\vartheta)$ is a multibranched function as
(5.75) \begin{equation} E(\vartheta) = N_{\mathrm{C}} M^2 \min_k \left\{f\left(\frac{\vartheta + 2\pi (k-1)}{N_{\rm C}}\right)\right\},\quad k=1,2,\cdots, N_{\rm C}.\label{eq5.75} \end{equation} 

Expanding $f(\vartheta) = f_0 + f_2 \vartheta ^2 + \cdots $ and considering that higher order terms in $\vartheta $ are suppressed at large $N_{\mathrm {C}}$, the vacuum energy at $\vartheta =0$ is given by
(5.76) \begin{equation}\label{eq5.76} E_k(0) = - \frac{N_{\mathrm{C}}}{3}M^2 + \frac{12\pi}{N_{\rm C}}M^2 (k-1)^2,\quad k=1,2,\cdots,N_{\rm C}, \end{equation} 

where we have set $f_0 = -1/3$ and $f_2 = 12\pi $ in such a way that Eqs. (5.70) and (5.71) are reproduced. Therefore, there exist $N_{\mathrm {C}}$ local minima among which only one $(k=1)$ is a genuine ground state while the others are quasi vacua. Each local minimum corresponds to a quantum vortex state. Namely, the genuine ground state $(k=1)$ is the stable vortex with $T_1 = T_{\mathrm {classical}} + E_1(0)$, and the quasi vacua with $k \ge 2$ are the metastable string with the tension $T_k = T_{\rm classical} + E_k(0)$.

Let us discuss the representations of the bound state of the monopole and anti-monopole. The physical degrees of freedom of $\phi $ are $2(N_{\mathrm {C}}-1)$ after fixing $U(1)$ gauge symmetry. For example, the inhomogeneous coordinates are
(5.77) \begin{equation}\label{eq5.77} \phi = \frac{1}{\sqrt {1+|b_1|^2}} \left(\begin{matrix} 1\\ b_1 \end{matrix} \right),\quad \phi = \frac{1}{\sqrt {1+|b_1|^2 + |b_2|^2}} \left(\begin{matrix} 1\\b_1\\b_2 \end{matrix}\right), \end{equation} 

for $N_{\mathrm {C}}=2$ and $N_{\mathrm {C}}=3$, respectively. For $N_{\rm C}=2$, $\phi $ ($\phi ^{\dagger }$) represents one (anti-)kink, as can be seen in Fig. 21(a). Each of them corresponds to one (anti-)monopole. For the case of the CFL phase with $N_{\mathrm {C}}=3$, one (anti-)monopole is a composite state of $N_{\mathrm {C}}-1=2$ (anti-)kinks, each of which has one complex moduli (position and phase), as seen in Fig. 21(b). Since the fields $\phi $ and $\phi ^{\dagger }$ transform as (anti-)fundamental representations under $SU(N_{\mathrm {C}})$, respectively, these (anti-)monopoles belong to $\textbf {N}_{\mathrm {C}}$ ($\textbf {N}_{\mathrm {C}}^*$) fundamental representations of $SU(N_{\mathrm {C}})$. The (anti-)monopoles are not free particles but appear as a mesonic bound state that belongs to the $\textbf {N}_{\mathrm {C}} \otimes \textbf {N}_{\rm C}^* = \textbf {1} \oplus \textbf {N}_{\rm C}^2-1$ representation. Note that it was shown in Refs. [384,385] that the singlet in this decomposition does not appear in the spectrum in the ${\mathbb {C}}P^1$ model ($N_{\mathrm {C}}=2$). This was interpreted in Ref. [234] as indicating that the singlet corresponds to a set of a monopole and an anti-monopole with opposite charges, which is unstable to decay. Although there is no such calculation for $N_{\mathrm {C}} \geq 3$, we expect that the same holds.

In this subsection, we discuss Yang–Mills instantons [48], classified by the third homotopy group of the gauge symmetry
(5.78) \begin{equation}\label{eq5.78} \pi_3[SU(3)_{\rm C}] \simeq {\mathbb{Z}}. \end{equation} 

The instanton number is $k = (8 \pi ^2 /g_{\mathrm {s}}^2) \int F \wedge F \in \pi _3[SU(3)_{\rm C}]$.

At asymptotic large $\mu $, bulk instanton effects with the energy $\sim 1/g_{\mathrm {s}}^2 \gg 1$ are highly suppressed due to the asymptotic freedom of QCD and the screening of Yang–Mills instantons [302,374]. As classical solutions, Yang–Mills instantons are unstable against shrinkage in the CFL ground state, which can be understood from Derrick’s scaling argument [88]. Instead, Yang–Mills instantons can exist stably inside a non-Abelian vortex [104,133,107]. They are lumps or sigma model instantons [276] supported by the second homotopy group
(5.79) \begin{equation}\label{eq5.79} \pi_2 ({\mathbb{C}}P^{2}) \simeq {\mathbb{Z}} \end{equation} 

in the ${\mathbb {C}}P^{2}$ model as the low-energy effective theory of a non-Abelian vortex.

Lump solutions exist inside a ${\mathbb {C}}P^1$ submanifold of the whole ${\mathbb {C}}P^{2}$. Let $z \in {\mathbb {C}}$ be the complex coordinate made of Wick-rotated coordinates of the vortex world-sheet. By using the projective coordinate $w$ of ${\mathbb {C}}P^1$, the lump solution can be written as
(5.80) \begin{equation}\label{eq5.80} w(z) = \sum_{a=1}^k \frac{\lambda_a}{z - z_a} \end{equation} 

with the size and phase moduli $\lambda _a \in {\mathbb {C}}^*$ and the position moduli $z_a \in {\mathbb {C}}$ of the $a$th instanton among $k$ instantons where $k \in \pi _2 ({\mathbb {C}}P^{2}) \simeq {\mathbb {Z}}$. The instanton energy in the vortex world-sheet is $C^{0,3} \sim (\mu /\Delta _{\mathrm {CFL}})^2 \gg 1/g_{\rm s}^2$ [see Eq. (5.18)], which is further suppressed more than the bulk instantons, consistent with the result in the last subsection. The fact that the instanton energy $C^{0,3}\sim (\mu /\Delta _{\mathrm {CFL}})^2$ inside the vortex is larger than the one $1/g_{\mathrm {s}}^2$ in the bulk implies that instantons are repulsive from vortices.

Now we discuss the properties of the interaction (6.3). First, let us look at the interaction of vortices with ${U}(1)_{{\mathrm {B}}}$ phonons. This part is essentially the same as a vortex in a superfluid. For a general vortex configuration we can write the Abelian component of the vorticity tensor as
(6.4) \begin{equation}\label{eq6.4} \left(\omega^0\right)_{\rho\sigma}(x) \equiv \frac{1}{2\pi}\epsilon_{\mu\nu\rho\sigma} \partial^\nu \partial^\mu \pi_{\rm MV}(x), \end{equation} 

where $\pi _{\mathrm {MV}}(x)$ is the multivalued part of the phase of the order parameter fields. The multivalued part is in general allowed, since it is a phase. Equation (6.4) appears to automatically vanish, but in fact it does not, since the two derivatives do not commute, which reflects the multivaluedness of $\pi _{\mathrm {MV}}(x)$. For a general vortex configuration, the vorticity tensor can be written as
(6.5) \begin{equation}\label{eq6.5} (\omega^0)^{\mu\nu}(x) =\frac{1}{N_{\rm C}}\int d\tau d \sigma \frac{\partial (X^\mu, X^\nu)}{\partial (\tau, \sigma) } \delta^{(4)} (x - X^\mu(\tau, \sigma)), \end{equation} 

where $N_{\mathrm {C}}$ is the number of colors ($N_{\mathrm {C}}=3$) and $X^\mu (\tau , \sigma)$ is the the space-time position of the vortex parametrized by world-sheet coordinates $\tau $ and $\sigma $. The interaction of vortices with ${U}(1)_{{\mathrm {B}}}$ phonons is rewritten as
(6.6) \begin{equation}\label{eq6.6} {\mathcal{S}}^{\rm Ph}_{\rm int} = -\frac{2\pi m^0}{N_{\rm C}} \int d \sigma^{\mu\nu} B^0_{\mu\nu}, \end{equation} 

where $d \sigma ^{\mu \nu } \equiv \frac {\partial (X^\mu ,X^\nu)}{\partial (\tau , \sigma)} d \tau d \sigma $ is an area element of the vortex world-sheet. The interaction (6.6) is a natural generalization of the gauge interaction of a point particle,
(6.7) \begin{equation}\label{eq6.7} \mathcal{S} = \int dx^\mu A_{\mu}. \end{equation} 

The factor $1/N_{\mathrm {C}}$, which is equal to the $U(1)_{\mathrm {B}}$ winding number of vortices with the lowest energy, reflects the fact that the strength of the interaction is proportional to the winding number with respect to ${U}(1)_{{\mathrm {B}}}$ symmetry. We also note that ${U}(1)_{{\mathrm {B}}}$ phonons $B_{\mu \nu }^0$ do not couple to the orientational zero modes. Phonons are blind to the orientation of a vortex.

